window.AIBRIEFS=window.AIBRIEFS||{};Object.assign(window.AIBRIEFS,{"1974/73-2024":{"facts":"Organizations and individuals from the Rochester, New York, area sued the neighboring town of Penfield and members of its zoning, planning and town boards, alleging that Penfield's zoning ordinance, by its terms and as enforced, excluded people of low and moderate income, and with them many members of racial and ethnic minorities, from living in the town. The ordinance allocated 98% of vacant land to single-family detached housing and only 0.3% to multifamily housing. The plaintiffs were Metro-Act of Rochester (a nonprofit), Rochester property taxpayers who said Penfield's practices raised their city taxes, and low- and moderate-income minority residents of the Rochester area who said they could not find housing in Penfield. Rochester Home Builders Association sought to intervene, and the Housing Council in the Monroe County Area sought to be added as a plaintiff. They claimed violations of the First, Ninth and Fourteenth Amendments and of 42 U.S.C. §§ 1981, 1982 and 1983, and sought declaratory and injunctive relief and damages.","procedure":"The District Court for the Western District of New York dismissed the complaint, holding among other things that none of the plaintiffs had standing, and denied the motions to add Housing Council and to let Home Builders intervene. The Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit affirmed on standing grounds, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did any of the plaintiffs, whether individuals, taxpayers or associations, allege facts sufficient to give them standing to challenge Penfield's allegedly exclusionary zoning practices in federal court?","holding":"No. None of the petitioners met the threshold requirement of clearly alleging facts showing that he was a proper party to invoke the court's power, whether standing is viewed as an Article III case-or-controversy requirement or as a matter of prudential limits on federal jurisdiction. A plaintiff challenging exclusionary zoning must allege specific, concrete facts showing that the practices harm him personally and that he would benefit in a tangible way from the court's intervention.","reasoning":"Justice Powell explained that “In essence the question of standing is whether the litigant is entitled to have the court decide the merits of the dispute or of particular issues,” a question with a constitutional dimension (a personal injury fairly traceable to the defendant and redressable by the court) and a prudential one (generally no standing for generalized grievances or to assert the rights of third parties, unless Congress provides otherwise). The low- and moderate-income plaintiffs had never lived in or owned property in Penfield and had not been denied any permit; their inability to live there depended on third-party builders, and the record suggested it reflected the economics of the housing market, so they failed to show a substantial probability that relief would let them live there. The Court stated: “We hold only that a plaintiff who seeks to challenge exclusionary zoning practices must allege specific, concrete facts demonstrating that the challenged practices harm him, and that he personally would benefit in a tangible way from the court's intervention.” The Rochester taxpayers' injury was conjectural, would result from decisions of Rochester officials who were not parties, and rested on the rights of excluded third parties, which prudential rules barred them from asserting. Metro-Act's claim that its Penfield members lost the benefits of an integrated community was distinguished from Trafficante v. Metropolitan Life Ins. Co. because no claim was made under the Civil Rights Act of 1968. Home Builders could not seek damages for its members without their individual participation and identified no current project blocked by the town; Housing Council's only member with a Penfield project, Penfield Better Homes, had not shown that its 1969 dispute remained live when the suit was filed in 1972.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented, writing that “the Court reads the complaint and the record with antagonistic eyes,” that Metro-Act and Housing Council had standing, and that the case should go to trial. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices White and Marshall, dissented, arguing that the opinion has overtones of outmoded notions of pleading and that its rejection of almost every kind of plaintiff “can be explained only by an indefensible hostility to the claim on the merits”; in his view at least three groups of plaintiffs had alleged enough to proceed.","disposition":"Affirmed, 5-4. Justice Powell delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Blackmun and Rehnquist. Justice Douglas dissented; Justice Brennan dissented, joined by Justices White and Marshall.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep422/usrep422490/usrep422490.pdf"},"1973/72-1410":{"facts":"Illinois officials administered Aid to the Aged, Blind, or Disabled (AABD), a program funded jointly by the State and the federal government. Federal regulations required states to decide applications within 30 or 45 days and to send checks within those periods, but Illinois's own manual and practice did not comply. Jordan, whose disability application went unacted on for almost four months, sued state and county officials for himself and a class, alleging violations of the federal regulations and of equal protection.","procedure":"The district court declared the Illinois manual provision invalid, enjoined future compliance with the federal time limits, and also ordered state officials to release and remit AABD benefits wrongfully withheld to eligible applicants from July 1, 1968, to April 1971. The Seventh Circuit affirmed, rejecting an Eleventh Amendment objection by treating the payments as “equitable restitution.” The Supreme Court granted certiorari because of a conflict with the Second Circuit.","question":"Does the Eleventh Amendment bar a federal court from ordering state officials to pay retroactive welfare benefits wrongfully withheld, and did Illinois waive its immunity by taking part in the federal-state program?","holding":"Yes. The Eleventh Amendment bars the retroactive portion of the decree because the award would be paid from the state treasury and is in practical effect damages against the State; Ex parte Young permits only prospective relief. Illinois did not waive its immunity merely by participating in a federally funded program.","reasoning":"Justice Rehnquist reviewed the history of the Eleventh Amendment and Hans v. Louisiana to show that an unconsenting State is immune from federal suits by its own citizens, and that a suit nominally against officials is barred when it is in essence one for money from the state treasury (Ford Motor Co.). Ex parte Young allows prospective injunctions, and their ancillary effect on state funds is permissible, but this award compensates for past violations and would to a virtual certainty be paid from state funds. Labeling it “equitable restitution” does not change that, and Ford Motor Co. would otherwise have to be overruled. The Court disapproved its earlier summary affirmances and Shapiro v. Thompson to the extent inconsistent, noting that summary dispositions carry less weight than an opinion on the merits. On waiver, the Court required express language or overwhelming implication, said “Constructive consent is not a doctrine commonly associated with the surrender of constitutional rights, and we see no place for it here,” and distinguished Parden and Petty because Congress here had not authorized suit against a class of defendants that includes States. Section 1983 does not itself waive immunity. The defense could be raised late because it partakes of a jurisdictional bar.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented: the case is governed by Ex parte Young, prospective and retroactive relief have the same effect on the treasury, many prior cases approved retroactive payments, and by joining the cooperative welfare program Illinois waived its immunity as in Parden. Justice Brennan dissented, adhering to his view that States surrendered sovereign immunity in the plan of the Convention for matters within Congress's enumerated powers, and noting that the Eleventh Amendment by its terms does not bar suits by a State's own citizens. Justice Marshall, joined by Justice Blackmun, dissented: by voluntarily joining a cooperative program and accepting its conditions, Illinois consented to the full range of section 1983 remedies, including retroactive payments, which Congress intended and which are needed to deter noncompliance.","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals was reversed as to the retroactive payments and the case remanded. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White and Powell; Justices Douglas, Brennan, Marshall and Blackmun dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep415/usrep415651/usrep415651.pdf"},"1976/75-1578":{"facts":"Sykes shot and killed Willie Gilbert and then told police and his wife what he had done. At the station he was read his Miranda rights, agreed to talk, and made a statement that two officers repeated at his Florida murder trial. His counsel never objected that he had been too intoxicated to understand the warnings, and the trial judge did not raise the issue or hold a hearing on voluntariness.","procedure":"Sykes was convicted of third-degree murder, did not challenge the statements on direct appeal, and later raised the claim without success in state post-conviction proceedings. The federal district court ordered a state hearing on voluntariness, and the Fifth Circuit agreed that the failure to comply with Florida's contemporaneous-objection rule barred review only if the right to object was deliberately bypassed for tactical reasons.","question":"Does a state defendant's failure to object at trial, as Florida's contemporaneous-objection rule requires, bar federal habeas corpus review of a Miranda claim unless the failure was a knowing and deliberate bypass under Fay v. Noia?","holding":"Yes. Absent a showing of cause for the noncompliance and actual prejudice from the alleged violation, a failure to make a timely objection under a state contemporaneous-objection rule bars federal habeas review of the underlying claim. The Court rejected the broad deliberate-bypass standard of Fay v. Noia as applied to such trial defaults.","reasoning":"Justice Rehnquist concluded that Florida's rule required a motion to suppress before trial and that nothing in Jackson v. Denno requires a voluntariness hearing without some challenge to the confession, so the default was an independent and adequate state ground. He extended the cause-and-prejudice test of Davis and Francis v. Henderson to this setting. A contemporaneous objection lets the record be made while witnesses' memories are fresh, lets the trial judge who saw the witnesses decide the facts, can lead to exclusion of the evidence and so promote finality, and forces the prosecution to confront the issue. The broad Fay rule could encourage defense lawyers to hold back claims and gamble on acquittal, weaken state enforcement of such rules, and turn the state trial from the main event into a “tryout on the road” for federal habeas. The cause-and-prejudice exception guards against a miscarriage of justice. The Court left its precise content open, but found neither here: Sykes gave no explanation for his failure to object, and the other evidence of guilt was substantial.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred, stressing that the Fay deliberate-bypass standard was designed for decisions personally made by the defendant, such as whether to appeal, and does not fit on-the-spot trial decisions that necessarily rest with counsel. Justice Stevens concurred, seeing the holding as consistent with how lower courts actually applied Fay and thinking competent counsel could have chosen not to object to this statement, so that collateral attack was unwarranted. Justice White concurred in the judgment: the Court's finding of no prejudice amounted to a harmless-error finding that sufficed, and he believed the bypass rule, applied to counsel's trial judgments, already protected the State, with the petitioner bearing the burden of explaining the default. Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, dissented: ordinary procedural defaults come from lawyer error or inadvertence, not tactics, so Fay's rule of treating only intentional forfeitures as binding should remain; the new test is undefined, the State's interests do not justify punishing clients for counsel's mistakes, and the result could leave constitutional claims with no court.","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals was reversed and the case remanded to the district court with instructions to dismiss the habeas petition. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices Stewart, Blackmun, Powell and Stevens.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep433/usrep433072/usrep433072.pdf"},"1972/71-1639":{"facts":"Oklahoma's merit system Act, modeled on the federal Hatch Act, barred classified state employees from soliciting contributions for political purposes, from serving on party committees or as officers of partisan clubs, from running for paid public office, and from taking part in party management or campaigns, while allowing them privately to express opinions and vote. Three employees of the Corporation Commission were charged with violating it by asking coworkers to do campaign work and, for two of them, soliciting money, for the re-election of their superior.","procedure":"Before the Personnel Board proceeded, the employees sued in federal district court seeking to have two paragraphs of the Act declared void on their face for vagueness and overbreadth. The three-judge district court upheld the provisions, and the Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction to consider the case with CSC v. Letter Carriers.","question":"Is the Oklahoma statute unconstitutionally vague or substantially overbroad on its face, so that employees whose own conduct it may validly reach can still have it invalidated because it might be applied to protected expression by others?","holding":"No. The statute gives adequate warning of what it forbids and is not vague. For overbreadth, particularly where conduct and not merely speech is involved, the overbreadth must be real and substantial in relation to the statute's plainly legitimate sweep; this statute is not substantially overbroad, and any improper applications can be handled case by case.","reasoning":"Justice White found the statute clear enough for ordinary people to understand, noting that appellants' conduct fell within its hard core and that the Personnel Board could give advance rulings. Generally a person to whom a law can constitutionally be applied cannot challenge it based on possible unconstitutional applications to others, since courts are not roving commissions. The First Amendment overbreadth doctrine is a limited exception that allows challenges because a broad law may chill others' protected speech, but it is “strong medicine” to be used sparingly and as a last resort, and it weakens as the law reaches conduct rather than pure speech and falls within otherwise valid regulation. The Court therefore required overbreadth to be “substantial as well” as real. Section 818 is an even-handed, noncensorial regulation of political activity, and under Letter Carriers much of what it forbids is clearly valid. The Board's and the Attorney General's constructions confined it to clearly partisan activity, and possible improper applications, such as to buttons or bumper stickers, do not require striking it down entirely.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented, relying on his Letter Carriers dissent: the First Amendment protects public as well as private political discourse by government employees, who have no watered-down rights, and the statute's reservation of only private expression shows it reaches public expression. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Stewart and Marshall, dissented: the Act's key terms are left almost undefined and the Board's rules, including one banning political buttons and bumper stickers, show it reaches protected conduct, so it should be invalid on its face; the new requirement of substantial overbreadth is undefined, treats conduct differently from speech without explanation, and effectively overrules Coates v. City of Cincinnati.","disposition":"The judgment of the district court was affirmed. Justice White wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices Blackmun, Powell and Rehnquist; Justices Douglas, Brennan, Stewart and Marshall dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep413/usrep413601/usrep413601.pdf"},"1972/71-1043":{"facts":"Heller managed a Greenwich Village movie theater showing a sexually explicit film. After police saw part of it, a New York criminal court judge went to a performance and watched the entire film, then signed a warrant to seize the film and warrants to arrest the theater manager, projectionist and ticket taker, without notifying anyone at the theater. Police seized the only copy, three reels, and arrested Heller. He made no pretrial motion for the film's return or to suppress it, and nothing in the record showed whether another copy existed.","procedure":"Heller was tried 47 days after the seizure, convicted of obscenity under New York Penal Law section 235.05, and argued that seizing the film without a prior adversary hearing violated the Fourteenth Amendment. The New York Court of Appeals affirmed, holding that a judicial determination before the warrant issued was enough, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May a judge who has viewed a film and found it obscene issue a valid warrant to seize it as evidence in a prosecution of the exhibitor without first holding an adversary hearing on probable obscenity?","holding":"Yes. Seizing a single copy of a film for the bona fide purpose of preserving it as evidence is constitutional if done under a warrant issued by a neutral magistrate on a finding of probable obscenity and a prompt adversary judicial determination is available on request. If other copies are unavailable, the film must be copied so exhibition can continue or be returned. The case was remanded on the substantive obscenity challenges in light of Miller and Paris Adult Theatre.","reasoning":"Chief Justice Burger observed that the Court had never recognized an absolute right to an adversary hearing before every seizure of allegedly obscene material, and that here the judge was a neutral magistrate who saw the entire film and could focus on the question of obscenity. Freedman v. Maryland and Thirty-seven Photographs require an adversary judicial determination for a valid final restraint, but only promptly, not before the initial seizure. Here the film was not subjected to a final restraint such as an injunction or destruction; one copy was held as evidence, there was no showing that exhibition was prevented, and an adversary trial occurred within 48 days. Any delay was due to Heller's choice not to file pretrial motions, though New York said it was ready to give immediate hearings. A Quantity of Books and Marcus involved mass seizures of publications for destruction without a judicial determination, and the Court did not disturb them, but seizing one copy as evidence is different. With a prior finding of probable cause and a prompt adversary hearing available, a preseizure hearing would add little protection, especially since films are compact and easily destroyed or altered. The Court remanded so New York courts could reconsider Heller's overbreadth, vagueness and private-viewing claims under Miller v. California and Paris Adult Theatre I v. Slaton.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented, as he would have reversed outright because he thought the obscenity statute itself violated the First Amendment. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Stewart and Marshall, dissented, concluding that the obscenity statute was unconstitutionally overbroad on its face for the reasons in his Paris Adult Theatre dissent, so that he did not reach the seizure question.","disposition":"The judgment of the New York Court of Appeals was vacated and the case remanded to reconsider the substantive challenges in light of Miller and Paris Adult Theatre I. Chief Justice Burger wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices White, Blackmun, Powell and Rehnquist.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep413/usrep413483/usrep413483.pdf"},"1971/70-71":{"facts":"Bass, previously convicted of a felony in New York, was found on separate occasions to possess a pistol and a shotgun. The federal indictment did not allege, and the prosecution made no attempt to show, that either gun was possessed in commerce or affecting commerce. The government proceeded on the view that the statute bans all possession and receipt of firearms by convicted felons with no showing of a connection to interstate commerce.","procedure":"Bass was convicted under section 1202(a) of the Omnibus Crime Control and Safe Streets Act. The Second Circuit reversed, doubting the statute's constitutionality if read as the government urged. The Supreme Court granted certiorari to resolve a conflict among the lower courts.","question":"Does the phrase “in commerce or affecting commerce” in section 1202(a) apply to the offenses of receiving and possessing a firearm, so that the government must show an interstate commerce connection in each case, or only to transporting?","holding":"The statute is ambiguous, and because it is criminal and the government's reading would significantly alter the federal-state balance, the narrower reading controls: the commerce requirement applies to possession and receipt as well as transportation, and the government must prove a nexus to interstate commerce in each case. The conviction could not stand without such proof.","reasoning":"Justice Marshall found the text and legislative history inconclusive. Grammatically, the natural reading is that the commerce clause qualifies all three verbs, and the government offered no sensible reason for limiting it to transportation while punishing all possession and receipt; the omitted comma carried no weight. The government's argument that the narrow reading would make Title VII redundant with Title IV was rejected, since the Titles overlap under either reading and Title VII was a hasty last-minute amendment. The legislative history, including Senator Long's remarks and the findings, leaned toward the government but did not explain why the commerce clause appears at all, and the Court concluded the statute was ambiguous (the government conceded the statute is “not a model of logic or clarity”). Two principles then decided the case: ambiguity in criminal statutes is resolved in favor of lenity, because of fair warning and because legislatures rather than courts should define crimes, and Congress is not deemed to have significantly changed the federal-state balance in policing local crime without a clear statement. The Court added that the nexus is met for receipt if the firearm previously traveled in interstate commerce, and for possession if it was moving interstate or its possession affects commerce. It did not reach the constitutional question.","separate":"Justice Brennan joined the judgment and opinion except for Part III, preferring to wait to decide what evidence suffices to show the nexus until a case presents it. Justice Blackmun, joined by the Chief Justice, dissented, arguing that the statute's structure and punctuation, the congressional findings and Senator Long's statements show Congress meant to reach every possession and receipt by a felon, that the Court's reading makes the statute nearly duplicate Title IV, and that the Court should have decided the constitutional issue.","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals reversing the conviction was affirmed. Justice Marshall wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Douglas, Stewart and White and, except for Part III, Justice Brennan; Justice Blackmun and the Chief Justice dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep404/usrep404336/usrep404336.pdf"},"1971/70-5106":{"facts":"Grayned took part in a demonstration of about 200 people in front of West Senior High School in Rockford, Illinois, after Black students' grievances went unanswered. Demonstrators marched on a sidewalk about 100 feet from the building with signs about Black teachers and counselors. Witnesses for the city said the demonstrators chanted and made noise audible in the school, distracted students, and disrupted school procedure; defense witnesses said the demonstration was quiet and orderly.","procedure":"Grayned was convicted of violating two Rockford ordinances, an antipicketing ordinance and an antinoise ordinance, and fined $25 on each. He challenged only their facial validity, and the Illinois Supreme Court upheld both. The Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction.","question":"Is the antipicketing ordinance valid, and is the antinoise ordinance, which bars willfully making a noise or diversion that disturbs or tends to disturb the peace or good order of a school session on adjacent grounds, unconstitutionally vague or overbroad on its face?","holding":"The antipicketing ordinance, which allowed peaceful labor picketing but no other picketing near schools, violates the Equal Protection Clause under Police Department of Chicago v. Mosley. The antinoise ordinance is neither vague nor overbroad, because it prohibits only actual or imminent, willful interference with normal school activity.","reasoning":"On the antipicketing ordinance, the Court relied on its Mosley decision of the same day. On vagueness, Justice Marshall stated that “It is a basic principle of due process that an enactment is void for vagueness if its prohibitions are not clearly defined,” because vague laws fail to give fair warning, invite arbitrary enforcement, and chill protected freedoms. Although the question was close, the Court could not narrow state laws but extrapolated the ordinance's meaning from its words, its school context, and Illinois decisions construing similar laws to require an imminent threat, and concluded that it prohibits only willful noise or diversion that actually or imminently disrupts normal school activity. Unlike the ordinances in Cox and Coates, it is tied to a measurable impact, requires causation and willfulness, and does not punish unpopular views. On overbreadth, public sidewalks may be used for expression but are subject to reasonable time, place and manner rules, and the question is whether the manner of expression is basically incompatible with the normal activity of a place at a particular time. Under Tinker v. Des Moines, expression may be barred near a school only if it materially disrupts classwork or involves substantial disorder, and the ordinance goes no further, leaving quiet picketing and picketing outside school sessions untouched.","separate":"Justice Blackmun joined the judgment and Part I and concurred in the result as to Part II. Justice Douglas joined Part I but dissented as to the antinoise ordinance, arguing that the record showed Grayned marched quietly and made no noise, that the demonstration was orderly and in the best First Amendment tradition, and that the disruption came from a racial issue properly addressed through First Amendment means; he rejected the Court's view that his as-applied question was not presented.","disposition":"The judgment of the Illinois Supreme Court was affirmed in part and reversed in part: the antipicketing conviction was reversed and the antinoise ordinance upheld. Justice Marshall wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices Brennan, Stewart, White, Powell and Rehnquist.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep408/usrep408104/usrep408104.pdf"},"1977/76-1346":{"facts":"Respondent Pons sued her former employer, Lorillard, alleging she was discharged because of her age in violation of the Age Discrimination in Employment Act of 1967 (ADEA). She sought reinstatement, lost wages, liquidated damages, attorney's fees, and costs. She demanded a jury trial on all issues of fact, and the employer moved to strike the demand.","procedure":"The District Court struck the jury demand but certified the question for interlocutory appeal. The Fourth Circuit vacated the order, holding that the ADEA and the Seventh Amendment gave respondent a jury trial right on her lost-wages claim. The Supreme Court granted certiorari to resolve a conflict among the circuits.","question":"Is there a right to a jury trial in a private civil action for lost wages under the Age Discrimination in Employment Act?","holding":"Yes. Although the ADEA does not expressly provide for jury trials, its structure shows that Congress intended a jury to be available in private ADEA actions when a party seeks one. The Court therefore did not reach the Seventh Amendment question.","reasoning":"The Court first took up the statutory question to avoid a constitutional one. Section 7(b) directs that the ADEA be enforced under the powers, remedies, and procedures of the Fair Labor Standards Act (FLSA), and before 1967 every court to consider the question had held that private FLSA actions carried a jury right. Congress is presumed to know such an interpretation when it incorporates a prior statute. Its selective changes to FLSA features, such as allowing injunctions and limiting liquidated damages to willful violations, showed detailed knowledge and suggested that what it did not change was meant to be adopted. The ADEA also authorizes “legal or equitable relief,” and “legal” is a term of art that ordinarily implies a jury under the Seventh Amendment. Comparison with Title VII did not help the petitioner, because Title VII does not use the word “legal,” makes backpay a matter of equitable discretion, and Congress chose FLSA procedures over Title VII procedures for the ADEA.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Marshall wrote for the Court; all participating Justices joined, and Justice Blackmun took no part. The Court expressed no view on a jury right for liquidated damages.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep434/usrep434575/usrep434575.pdf"},"1970/1066":{"facts":"Overton Park is a 342-acre city park in Memphis containing a zoo, golf course, nature trails, and 170 acres of forest. The Secretary of Transportation approved federal funding for a six-lane section of Interstate 40 that would cut through the park, sever the zoo from the rest, and destroy 26 acres. Section 4(f) of the Department of Transportation Act of 1966 and section 138 of the Federal-Aid Highway Act of 1968 bar such approval unless there is no feasible and prudent alternative and the project includes all possible planning to minimize harm. The Secretary announced his concurrence in 1968 and gave final route and design approval in November 1969, with no statement of factual findings explaining why alternatives were rejected.","procedure":"Citizens and conservation groups sued to block the project. The District Court granted summary judgment for the Secretary on the strength of litigation affidavits, and the Sixth Circuit affirmed, both holding that formal findings were not required and refusing to depose a former Federal Highway Administrator. The Supreme Court stayed construction and granted certiorari.","question":"Is the Secretary's approval of a highway through a public park subject to judicial review under the Administrative Procedure Act, and if so, what standard applies and what record must the court review?","holding":"The Secretary's decision is reviewable because it is not committed to agency discretion by law, and the proper standard is the arbitrary-and-capricious test of section 706(2)(A) applied through a thorough, probing inquiry rather than de novo or substantial-evidence review. Formal findings were not required, but review on litigation affidavits was inadequate, so the case was remanded to the District Court for plenary review of the full administrative record.","reasoning":"The exception for action committed to agency discretion is very narrow and covers cases where statutes are drawn so broadly that there is no law to apply. Section 4(f) and section 138 are clear directives that give protection of parkland paramount importance, so the Secretary may approve a route through a park only on finding that alternatives are infeasible or present unique problems, leaving law to apply. The substantial-evidence and de novo standards apply only in rulemaking, formal adjudication, or specific circumstances not present here. Review must nevertheless be searching: the court asks whether the Secretary acted within his authority, whether his choice rested on relevant factors without a clear error of judgment, and whether required procedures were followed, though the court may not substitute its judgment for the agency's. Post hoc affidavits prepared for litigation are not the whole record. Because the record may not reveal the Secretary's reasoning, the District Court may require explanatory testimony from the decisionmakers or new formal findings from the Secretary, viewed critically.","separate":"Justice Black, joined by Justice Brennan, agreed that the judgment should be reversed but would remand to the Secretary of Transportation rather than the District Court, because the record showed he had not complied with Congress's command. Justice Blackmun joined the Court's opinion and added a brief statement that the decisions under attack were made over more than a decade by several officials and that the statutes were enacted after the project was well developed, which explains the sketchy record.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded to the District Court. Justice Marshall wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices Harlan, Stewart, White, and Blackmun; Justice Douglas took no part.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep401/usrep401402/usrep401402.pdf"},"1976/74-6593":{"facts":"Gardner was convicted of first-degree murder in Florida for killing his wife with a blunt instrument. At the separate sentencing hearing, he testified to a day-long drinking spree and almost no memory of the assault, and the jury advised a life sentence because mitigating circumstances outweighed the aggravating one. The trial judge, who had ordered a presentence investigation report, rejected that advice and imposed death, finding the crime especially heinous, atrocious, or cruel and no mitigating circumstance. The judge said he relied in part on the report, but part of it was confidential and was not disclosed to counsel for either side, and counsel never asked to see it.","procedure":"The Florida Supreme Court affirmed the death sentence over two dissents, without discussing Gardner's objection to the confidential report and without having the confidential portion in the appellate record. The Supreme Court granted certiorari after upholding Florida's capital-sentencing statute on its face in Proffitt v. Florida.","question":"Does a death sentence violate due process when it is imposed in part on a presentence report whose confidential portion was never disclosed to the defendant or his counsel or made part of the record on appeal?","holding":"Yes, in the judgment of the Court. A plurality concluded that Gardner was denied due process because the death sentence rested at least in part on information he had no opportunity to deny or explain. The death sentence was vacated and the case remanded for further proceedings in the trial court.","reasoning":"The plurality distinguished Williams v. New York, where the judge described the report's facts in open court and the defendant did not challenge them. Since 1949 the Court had recognized that death is a different kind of punishment and that sentencing must satisfy due process. The State's justifications did not hold up: confidentiality invites unreliable information, claimed delay is overstated and worth any cost in a capital case, rehabilitation concerns have no force when death is imposed, and trusting judges is foreclosed after Furman and ignores the value of adversary comment. Even if some material could be withheld for good cause, the full report would have to be in the appellate record so reviewing courts can see what motivated each death sentence. Counsel's failure to ask for the report was not an effective waiver. Remand only to supplement the record would not cure the error, because full disclosure might have led the judge to accept the jury's recommendation of life.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred in the judgment. Justice White concurred in the judgment on Eighth Amendment grounds, reasoning that a death-selection procedure allowing secret information fails the reliability that Woodson v. North Carolina requires, and he did not reach due process. Justice Blackmun concurred in the judgment based on Woodson and Roberts v. Louisiana. Justice Brennan agreed with the plurality's due process reasoning but would hold the death penalty always unconstitutional and so dissented from the remand. Justice Marshall dissented in part, also viewing the death penalty as always unconstitutional, and criticized the Florida courts' cursory review as undermining the premises of Proffitt. Justice Rehnquist dissented, arguing that a procedure not unfair under due process cannot make a sentence cruel and unusual, and would affirm.","disposition":"Vacated and remanded to the Florida Supreme Court with directions to order further trial-court proceedings. Justice Stevens announced the judgment and wrote an opinion joined by Justices Stewart and Powell, and other Justices concurred in the judgment or dissented as described.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep430/usrep430349/usrep430349.pdf"},"1978/78-5283":{"facts":"Jackson was convicted after a bench trial in Virginia of the first-degree murder of Mary Cole, a jail staff member who had befriended him. He did not dispute that he shot her twice at close range with his revolver. He told police that the shooting was accidental, a result of a struggle after she attacked him with a knife, and he also claimed he was too intoxicated to premeditate. The prosecution showed that he had been drinking and shooting at targets, had said he and the victim were about to engage in sexual activity, reloaded after firing warning shots, and drove to North Carolina after the shooting.","procedure":"The Virginia courts rejected his claim that the evidence of premeditation was insufficient. In federal habeas proceedings, the District Court applied the Thompson v. Louisville no-evidence test, found no evidence of premeditation, and granted the writ. The Fourth Circuit applying the same test reversed, finding some evidence of intent, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"In a federal habeas corpus proceeding challenging a state conviction as based on insufficient evidence, must the court ask only whether there was any evidence of guilt, or whether the evidence was enough for a rational trier of fact to find guilt beyond a reasonable doubt?","holding":"The court must ask whether, viewing the evidence in the light most favorable to the prosecution, any rational trier of fact could have found the essential elements of the crime beyond a reasonable doubt. A state prisoner is entitled to habeas relief under section 2254 if no rational trier of fact could have found guilt, and on this record a rational factfinder could have found first-degree murder.","reasoning":"In re Winship established that due process forbids conviction except on proof beyond a reasonable doubt of every element, and that requirement means little unless the factfinder applies it rationally to the evidence. The Thompson no-evidence rule protects only against wholly arbitrary convictions, and a mere modicum of evidence could satisfy it without supporting a rational finding of guilt beyond a reasonable doubt. The reviewing court does not ask whether it itself believes guilt was proved, and it respects the factfinder's role in resolving conflicts, weighing evidence, and drawing inferences, presuming conflicts were resolved for the prosecution. The Court rejected arguments that such claims should be barred or limited as in Stone v. Powell, because insufficiency goes to guilt or innocence, the burden on federal courts was exaggerated, and section 2254 makes federal courts responsible for state convictions that violate the Constitution. Applying the standard, the double shooting at close range, the reloading, the drive to North Carolina inconsistent with extreme intoxication, and the prior statements permitted a finding of premeditation.","separate":"Justice Stevens, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justice Rehnquist, concurred in the judgment. He argued that the new rule was unnecessary to decide the case because the evidence was sufficient under any standard, that Winship concerns the trial factfinder's state of mind and does not compel a particular appellate or habeas standard, and that the rule would burden federal and state judges with duplicative review for little benefit.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, White, Marshall, and Blackmun; Justice Powell took no part.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep443/usrep443307/usrep443307.pdf"},"1969/57":{"facts":"In January 1960 three or four masked men robbed six poker players in a Missouri basement. Ashe was charged separately with robbing each player. At his first trial, for robbing Knight, the proof that a robbery occurred was unassailable, but the evidence that Ashe was one of the robbers was weak, and the defense offered no testimony. The jury returned a verdict of not guilty due to insufficient evidence. Six weeks later Ashe was tried for robbing another player, Roberts. The witnesses were largely the same, but their identification testimony was now stronger and the State dropped one unhelpful witness. This time the jury convicted, and he received a 35-year sentence.","procedure":"The Missouri Supreme Court affirmed the conviction and rejected the former-jeopardy plea, and a state collateral attack failed. The federal District Court denied habeas relief, as did the Eighth Circuit, both relying on Hoag v. New Jersey. Benton v. Maryland, decided after those rulings, applied the Fifth Amendment's Double Jeopardy Clause to the States, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the Double Jeopardy Clause, applied to the States, bar a second trial for the robbery of a different victim when a jury in the first trial acquitted the defendant by determining that he was not one of the robbers?","holding":"Yes. The Fifth Amendment guarantee against double jeopardy incorporates the rule of collateral estoppel, which bars relitigation between the same parties of an issue of ultimate fact already determined by a valid and final judgment. Because the first jury necessarily decided that Ashe was not one of the robbers, Missouri could not constitutionally try him again on that issue.","reasoning":"Hoag v. New Jersey had asked only whether the second prosecution was fundamentally unfair under due process. After Benton, the question became whether collateral estoppel is part of the Double Jeopardy Clause, which is a matter of constitutional fact decided on the whole record. Collateral estoppel has long been a rule of federal criminal law and is applied with realism, not hypertechnically: where the earlier verdict was general, a court examines the pleadings, evidence, and charge and asks whether a rational jury could have based its verdict on an issue other than the one the defendant seeks to foreclose. Here the only rationally conceivable contested issue was whether Ashe was one of the robbers, and the jury resolved it in his favor. The Court reasoned that whatever else the Clause covers, it protects an acquitted person from having to run the gantlet a second time, and the identity of the second victim had no bearing on that issue. The State had conceded that it treated the first trial as a dry run for the second, which the Clause forbids.","separate":"Justice Black joined the opinion but rejected any suggestion that a fundamental-fairness test could substitute for the Bill of Rights. Justice Harlan joined, noting that he would have followed Hoag under traditional due process but accepted Benton's incorporation, and that the opinion does not embrace a same-transaction concept. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Douglas and Marshall, concurred and would also hold that the Clause requires the prosecution to join in one trial all charges arising from a single criminal episode. Chief Justice Burger dissented, arguing that the charges involved separate offenses against separate victims under the same-evidence test, that collateral estoppel is poorly suited to criminal cases and was extended on slogans, that the first jury's basis for acquittal was uncertain, and that the same-transaction idea would give later crimes free passes.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded to the Court of Appeals. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court; Justices Black, Harlan, and Brennan (joined by Justices Douglas and Marshall) concurred, and Chief Justice Burger dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep397/usrep397436/usrep397436.pdf"},"1973/73-507":{"facts":"Petitioners, including Hamling, Kemp, Wright, Thomas and two corporations, produced and sold an illustrated version of the official report of the President's Commission on Obscenity and Pornography. To advertise it they mailed to about 55,000 people a brochure with a collage of explicit photographs of heterosexual and homosexual acts and other sexual conduct, together with an order form and text praising the book. The individual petitioners were officers of the corporate petitioners, and evidence tied each of them to the mailing or to the sale of the report.","procedure":"A federal grand jury in the Southern District of California indicted petitioners under 18 U.S.C. sections 2, 371 and 1461. The jury convicted them on twelve counts of mailing and conspiring to mail the obscene advertisement but could not agree on counts concerning the illustrated report itself. The Ninth Circuit affirmed, and after this Court decided Miller v. California it denied rehearing, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Do the obscenity standards of Miller v. California require reversal of convictions obtained before Miller under the Memoirs test, and did the indictment, instructions, and trial rulings otherwise afford petitioners a fair trial under the Constitution?","holding":"No. Section 1461, read as limited to the kind of patently offensive hard-core sexual depictions described in Miller, is not unconstitutionally vague, and the brochure was plainly within that category. The instructions, the indictment, and the trial and jury-selection rulings were not reversible error, so the convictions stood.","reasoning":"Defendants whose convictions were on direct appeal when Miller was decided are entitled to whatever benefit Miller gives them, but the Court found none that required reversal. Miller rejected a uniform national standard as a constitutional requirement without requiring any particular geographic community, so reference to the nation as a whole in the instruction was not reversible absent a probability it affected the jury. Miller's examples of proscribable conduct gave a clarifying gloss to section 1461 rather than creating new crimes, so the statute gave adequate notice, and rejecting the Memoirs social-value test did not make the statute vague. The indictment, which used the statutory term obscene, a legal term of art with a settled meaning, gave sufficient notice. It was enough on scienter that petitioners knew the contents and character of the material they mailed, whatever they believed about its legal status. The Court found no abuse of discretion in excluding allegedly comparable materials, in instructing that the jury could consider appeal to deviant groups and pandering, or in the jury-selection and voir dire rulings, and it found no prejudice from the judge's failure to follow Rule 30 on objections to instructions.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented, reasoning that the First Amendment embodies the people's right to know, so that if officials may report on obscenity, nothing allows barring a glossary that factually illustrates the report. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Stewart and Marshall, dissented, adhering to the view that the government may not wholly suppress sexually oriented materials, so that section 1461 as construed is unconstitutionally overbroad and invalid on its face. He also argued that the Court wrongly overruled the long-accepted national standard of decency under the statute in favor of a local one and found other grounds for setting the convictions aside.","disposition":"Affirmed, by a 5-4 vote, in an opinion by Justice Rehnquist.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep418/usrep418087/usrep418087.pdf"},"1976/75-636":{"facts":"The United States sued T.I.M.E.-D.C., a nationwide motor freight carrier, and the International Brotherhood of Teamsters under Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964. It alleged that the company discriminated against Black and Spanish-surnamed employees by steering them into lower-paying city driver and serviceman jobs while line-driver jobs went to whites. It also alleged that the collective-bargaining seniority system locked in the effects of this discrimination, because a city driver who transferred to a line-driver job forfeited all accumulated seniority and started at the bottom of the line drivers' board. The government sought a general injunction and make-whole relief for individual victims.","procedure":"After a trial, the district court held that the company and the union had violated Title VII, enjoined both, and defined an affected class of minority employees entitled to preference for line-driver jobs, with three subclasses receiving different seniority relief. The Fifth Circuit agreed on liability but replaced the remedial scheme with a qualification-date formula and shortened the priority of laid-off line drivers. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did the company engage in a pattern or practice of discrimination, did the union violate Title VII by maintaining a seniority system that perpetuated past discrimination, and what individual relief may victims receive?","holding":"The company violated Title VII through a systemwide pattern or practice of discrimination, supported by statistical disparities and individual testimony. The seniority system was protected by section 703(h) because it was bona fide and not adopted with discriminatory intent, so the union did not violate the Act and the injunction against it was vacated. Victims of post-Act discrimination, including nonapplicants who can prove they were deterred by the discriminatory policy, may receive relief including retroactive seniority, but not to a date before the Act's effective date.","reasoning":"Where the government showed that discrimination was the company's standard operating procedure, the pervasive statistical disparities, bolstered by specific instances of discrimination, were not adequately rebutted and supported the findings below. Because a post-Act pattern was proved, retroactive seniority may be awarded as relief even if the seniority agreement does not provide for it. By the language and legislative history of section 703(h), Congress did not intend to make it unlawful for employees with vested seniority rights to keep exercising them even at the expense of pre-Act discriminatees, so a bona fide system that merely perpetuates pre-Act discrimination is lawful, and this system applied equally to all and was free from discriminatory purpose. Every post-Act minority applicant for line-driver work is presumptively entitled to relief unless the company shows its refusal was not discriminatory. A nonapplicant is not barred from relief because a consistently enforced discriminatory policy deters applications, but the government must prove at a remedial hearing which nonapplicants would have applied but for that policy. The district court must then balance the victims' interests against the legitimate expectations of innocent employees.","separate":"Justice Marshall, joined by Justice Brennan, concurred in part and dissented in part. He agreed with parts of the Court's analysis but argued that section 703(h) does not plainly and unmistakably protect seniority systems that perpetuate the effects of intentional past discrimination, since such differences result from prior discrimination, and that the legislative history and later administrative and legislative developments do not show Congress meant to legalize them.","disposition":"The Fifth Circuit's judgment was vacated and the case remanded for further proceedings, in an opinion by Justice Stewart joined by six other Justices, with Justices Marshall and Brennan concurring in part and dissenting in part.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep431/usrep431324/usrep431324.pdf"},"1969/79":{"facts":"Sandra Adickes, a white teacher from New York, took six Black students from a Mississippi Freedom School to the Hattiesburg public library, where they were turned away, and then to a S. H. Kress and Company store for lunch. A waitress served the students but refused to serve her, and when she left the store a Hattiesburg police officer arrested her for vagrancy. She alleged that a policeman had been in the store watching when service was refused. She sued Kress under 42 U.S.C. section 1983 for violating her Equal Protection right not to be discriminated against because of race.","procedure":"Her first count alleged refusal of service under a community custom of segregating the races in eating places, and her second alleged a conspiracy between Kress and the police. The district court granted summary judgment on the conspiracy count for want of alleged facts, and directed a verdict on the custom count because she could not prove a specific state-enforced custom of refusing to serve whites accompanied by Black people. The Second Circuit affirmed, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did the district court err in granting summary judgment on the conspiracy count, and what must a plaintiff show to establish under section 1983 that a private party refused service under color of a state custom?","holding":"Yes, summary judgment was improper because Kress did not carry its burden of showing no genuine issue of material fact about a conspiracy with the police. On the custom count, a plaintiff makes out a section 1983 equal protection claim by proving she was refused service because of a state-enforced custom of racial segregation in restaurants, and she need not prove a specific custom against serving whites with Black people or a statewide custom.","reasoning":"Section 1983 requires deprivation of a constitutional right and action under color of state law. A private party who is a willful participant in joint activity with state officials, such as a conspiracy with a policeman, acts under color of law, whether or not the officer's acts were authorized. On the record Kress did not rule out that a policeman was in the store, from which a jury could infer an understanding that she not be served, so it failed its initial burden as the movant and she was not required to produce opposing affidavits. As to custom, the statutory words and history mean that the custom or usage must have the force of law through the persistent practices of state officials, and it is not limited to customs enforced by a statute. A private person who discriminates racially knowing of and pursuant to such a state-enforced custom of segregation acts under color of that custom. The relevant custom is the longstanding, still prevailing, state-enforced segregation of public eating places, not a narrow practice about whites accompanied by Black people, and a custom with the force of law in a locality can violate the Fourteenth Amendment without being statewide.","separate":"Justice Black concurred in the judgment, concluding that on this record both the conspiracy and custom claims should have gone to the jury, even assuming the trial court's reading of custom or usage was correct. Justice Douglas dissented in part, arguing that the statutory words custom or usage were seriously emasculated by requiring that a custom have the force of law and be enforced by the State. Justice Brennan concurred in part and dissented in part, joining the Court's treatment of the conspiracy count but disagreeing with its reading of the under-color-of-law element and its approach to the custom claim, and addressing the standard for punitive damages. Justice Marshall took no part.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for further proceedings, in an opinion by Justice Harlan.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep398/usrep398144/usrep398144.pdf"},"1973/72-914":{"facts":"Personal representatives of the estates of three students killed at Kent State University in May 1970 sued the Governor of Ohio, the Adjutant General and his assistant, various officers and enlisted members of the Ohio National Guard, and the university president. They alleged that the defendants, acting under color of state law, “intentionally, recklessly, willfully and wantonly” caused an unnecessary Guard deployment on campus and ordered Guard members to perform illegal acts that caused the deaths. Fairly read, the complaints alleged that each defendant acted outside the scope of office or, within it, arbitrarily and in gross abuse of lawful powers.","procedure":"The complaints sought damages under 42 U.S.C. § 1983, with Ohio-law claims added through pendent or diversity jurisdiction. The district court dismissed for lack of jurisdiction, before any answer, on the theory that the suits were in effect against Ohio and barred by the Eleventh Amendment. The Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit affirmed on that ground and alternatively on the ground that executive immunity was absolute.","question":"Were the damages actions against state officials barred by the Eleventh Amendment, or by an absolute executive immunity, so that dismissal at the pleading stage was proper?","holding":"No. The Eleventh Amendment does not bar damages suits seeking to impose personal liability on state officials for deprivation of federal rights under color of state law, and state executive officers have only a qualified immunity whose scope depends on the office and the circumstances. Dismissal on the pleadings was premature.","reasoning":"On a motion to dismiss the question is whether the plaintiff may offer evidence, and the complaints are read favorably to the pleader, citing Conley v. Gibson. Under Ex parte Young, the Eleventh Amendment is no shield for an official accused of depriving another of a federal right under color of state law, and the complaints sought individual and personal liability rather than payment from the state treasury. As to immunity, Section 1983 reaches misuse of state authority, and its history shows no intent to abolish common-law immunities wholesale, but officials as a class cannot be totally exempt. Immunity rests on the injustice of liability for officers who must exercise discretion and the danger of deterring decisive action. A qualified immunity is available to executive officers, varying with the scope of discretion and responsibilities of the office and all circumstances as they reasonably appeared, together with good-faith belief. The lower courts wrongly accepted the Governor's good faith and took judicial notice of mob rule without any evidence, so further proceedings by summary judgment or trial were required.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for further proceedings. Chief Justice Burger delivered the opinion for a unanimous Court; Justice Douglas took no part.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep416/usrep416232/usrep416232.pdf"},"1978/77-926":{"facts":"Geraldine Cannon, a 39-year-old woman, applied to the medical schools of the University of Chicago and Northwestern University, both private institutions that receive federal financial assistance, and was rejected by both. She alleged that she was qualified, that less qualified applicants were admitted, and that the schools' policies against admitting applicants over 30 or 35 excluded women, who more often interrupt their education, without predicting success in medicine. After HEW did not act on her administrative complaint, she sued the schools, claiming among other things sex discrimination in violation of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972. The Court took the complaints' allegations as true because the case came up on motions to dismiss.","procedure":"The District Court dismissed the complaints because Title IX does not expressly authorize a private suit and the court declined to infer one. The Seventh Circuit affirmed, reasoning that Congress meant the fund-termination procedure in section 902 to be the exclusive enforcement method and that Title VI, the model for Title IX, carried no implied private action; it adhered to this view on rehearing after Congress passed the 1976 attorney's fees amendment. The Supreme Court granted certiorari on the Title IX question alone.","question":"Does Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, which prohibits sex discrimination in federally funded education programs but does not expressly say so, give a person discriminated against a private right of action against the institution?","holding":"Yes. Applying the four factors from Cort v. Ash, the Court held that Title IX implies a private cause of action for victims of the prohibited discrimination, so Cannon may maintain her suit despite the lack of express authorization in the statute.","reasoning":"First, Title IX is phrased in terms of the persons it protects, and a statute that creates a personal right to be free of discrimination is the kind from which the Court has regularly implied a remedy; Cannon belongs to the protected class. Second, the legislative history points toward a remedy: Title IX was copied from Title VI, its drafters expected it to be enforced as Title VI had been, and by 1972 lower courts had already read Title VI to permit private suits. Congress also showed its understanding in the attorney's fees provision of the 1972 Act and in later fees legislation, and it did not act to change the prevailing assumption. Third, a private action serves the statute's two aims, avoiding the use of federal money for discrimination and protecting individuals, because the fund cutoff is a severe remedy poorly suited to an isolated violation and HEW itself says it lacks the resources to police every complaint and supports the private remedy. Fourth, discrimination is not a matter traditionally left to the states, and the use of federal funds is the basis for the prohibition. The universities' argument that suits over admissions are burdensome is a policy objection that the Congresses enacting Titles VI and IX rejected, and nothing shows that private Title VI suits have overburdened schools or courts. Arguments drawn from the express remedies in other titles of the 1964 Act and from isolated remarks in the Title VI debates do not show an intent to exclude a private remedy.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred in the judgment without opinion. Justice Rehnquist, joined by Justice Stewart, concurred, saying that the question is one of statutory construction, that Congress in the era of the Civil Rights Act relied on courts to decide whether to allow private actions, and that Congress should now specify when it wants one, so the Court should be extremely reluctant to imply causes of action in future. Justice White, joined by Justice Blackmun, dissented, arguing that the history of Title VI and Title IX shows Congress meant enforcement to be carried out by federal agencies, with private suits against state actors already available under section 1983 and none intended against private recipients; he found the lower-court precedents, the fees provision and the cited Court cases inapposite and said the Court's remedy displaces the statutory scheme. Justice Powell dissented, agreeing with White that no private action was intended, and argued further that the Cort analysis violates separation of powers and Article III by letting courts create remedies that belong to Congress, and should be abandoned in favor of requiring the most compelling evidence of congressional intent.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Stevens wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, Stewart, Marshall and Rehnquist.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep441/usrep441677/usrep441677.pdf"},"1975/74-1124":{"facts":"Several low-income individuals and organizations representing low-income people sued the Secretary of the Treasury and the Commissioner of Internal Revenue over Revenue Ruling 69-545. That ruling dropped the earlier requirement that a nonprofit hospital serve patients unable to pay, to the extent of its financial ability, in order to qualify as charitable and tax exempt, and it held that a hospital offering only emergency care to indigents could qualify. The individual plaintiffs described occasions when they were refused hospital services or billed despite their indigency at hospitals that had been held tax exempt and received substantial private contributions. They alleged that the ruling encouraged hospitals to deny services to the poor, that it misread the Internal Revenue Code, and that it was issued without the notice and comment procedures of the Administrative Procedure Act. No hospital was a defendant.","procedure":"The District Court denied the government's motion to dismiss, which challenged standing, and on summary judgment held the ruling void as contrary to the Code. The D.C. Circuit also found jurisdiction but reversed on the merits, upholding the ruling as a permissible reading of the Code and as an interpretive rule exempt from rulemaking procedures. The Supreme Court granted both sides' petitions and consolidated them, and decided only the government's jurisdictional challenge.","question":"Did the indigent plaintiffs and the organizations representing them allege an injury fairly traceable to the Treasury officials' issuance of Revenue Ruling 69-545 and likely to be redressed by a favorable decision, as Article III standing requires?","holding":"No. The plaintiffs lacked standing because it was speculative whether the hospitals' denials of service resulted from the ruling rather than from the hospitals' own independent decisions, and speculative whether the relief sought would lead hospitals to serve the plaintiffs. The complaint should have been dismissed.","reasoning":"Standing asks whether the plaintiff has shown an injury to himself that is likely to be redressed by a favorable decision, and a federal court acting without that showing would exceed its Article III role. The organizations alleged no injury to themselves and could not rest standing on an abstract interest in the health of the poor; they could sue only as representatives of members actually injured. The plaintiffs did suffer injury in the sense of being denied hospital care, but the injury came from the hospitals, which were not before the court, and a federal court may redress only injury fairly traceable to the defendant rather than to independent third-party action. The allegation that the ruling encouraged hospitals to refuse indigents left it speculative whether the refusals stemmed from the ruling or from the hospitals' own choices. It was also speculative whether a court order would produce service for these plaintiffs, since hospitals might give up the tax benefits instead; the allegation that the hospitals received substantial deductible contributions did not show dependence on them. Linda R. S. v. Richard D. and Warth v. Seldin control, and United States v. SCRAP and Data Processing are distinguishable because the plaintiffs there alleged injury directly traceable to the challenged action. The Court did not reach whether a third party may ever challenge the IRS treatment of another or whether statutory or sovereign immunity bars applied.","separate":"Justice Stewart filed a concurring statement joining the opinion and adding that, outside the First Amendment area, he could not imagine a case in which a person whose own tax liability was unaffected could have standing to litigate someone else's tax liability. Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, concurred in the judgment but rejected the Court's standing reasoning. He would have decided the case on the ground that the challenge to the ruling was unripe or unsupported because the ruling on its face applies to a narrow category of hospitals and the plaintiffs never showed that the hospitals affecting them fell within it, so the government was entitled to summary judgment. He argued that the Court wrongly imposed demanding pleading and proof requirements on the chain of causation, conflicted with Data Processing, Barlow and SCRAP, and wrongly put its holding on the Article III minimum, beyond congressional power to correct.","disposition":"Judgment of the Court of Appeals vacated and the case remanded to the District Court with instructions to dismiss the complaint. Justice Powell wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White, Blackmun and Rehnquist; Justice Stevens took no part.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep426/usrep426026/usrep426026.pdf"},"1971/70-40":{"facts":"Georgia's 1968 abortion statute, modeled on the American Law Institute's Model Penal Code, made abortion a crime punishable by one to ten years in prison unless performed by a licensed Georgia physician who judged an abortion necessary because the pregnancy endangered the woman's life or seriously and permanently injured her health, the fetus would likely be born with a grave defect, or the pregnancy resulted from rape. The statute also required that the woman be a Georgia resident, that the abortion take place in a hospital accredited by the Joint Commission on Accreditation of Hospitals, that a hospital staff abortion committee approve it in advance, and that two other licensed physicians examine the woman and concur in the performing physician's judgment. Mary Doe, a pseudonym for a poor, married Georgia woman who was about eight weeks pregnant, already had three children and could not support another. Her request for an abortion was denied by a hospital committee because her situation did not fit the statutory grounds.","procedure":"Doe, other individuals including physicians, nurses, clergy and social workers, and two nonprofit corporations sued Georgia officials in federal court for a declaration that the statute was unconstitutional and for an injunction. A three-judge District Court held that all plaintiffs had standing but that only Doe presented a justiciable controversy, struck down the limit to three specified grounds and a few other provisions as infringing privacy, upheld the remaining procedural requirements, and denied an injunction. The plaintiffs appealed directly to the Supreme Court, and the case was argued and reargued alongside Roe v. Wade.","question":"Do the Georgia abortion statute's requirements of a physician's best clinical judgment, accredited-hospital performance, hospital committee approval, concurrence of two other physicians, and state residence, violate the Constitution?","holding":"The requirement that an abortion rest on the physician's best clinical judgment is not unconstitutionally vague. But the requirements of Joint Commission accreditation of the hospital, hospital committee approval, confirmation by two other physicians, and Georgia residence violate the Fourteenth Amendment. The woman's right to an abortion is not absolute, as stated in Roe v. Wade.","reasoning":"Under Roe v. Wade, Doe's case is live and she has standing, and the physician plaintiffs, who are the people the criminal statute directly threatens, may sue without first being prosecuted; the Court did not need to decide the standing of the other plaintiffs. The best-clinical-judgment standard is not vague because, as in United States v. Vuitch, physicians routinely make judgments of necessity, and the judgment may take account of physical, emotional, psychological and familial factors and age relevant to the patient's wellbeing. The accreditation requirement is unrelated to abortion as such and the State did not show that only hospitals, let alone accredited ones, can protect the patient's health; a hospital requirement that does not exclude the first trimester also fails under Roe. Committee approval is redundant because the medical judgment has already been made, it is not required for any other surgery, and it substantially limits the woman's and the physician's rights, while the hospital is already protected by its freedom to decline abortions and by conscience provisions. The two-physician confirmation has no rational connection with the patient's needs and unduly infringes the attending physician's right to practice, since a licensed physician is presumed competent to judge. The residence rule is not tied to preserving state facilities and, like the Privileges and Immunities Clause protection of those who enter a state to work, must protect persons who enter Georgia for medical services. With the three procedural requirements gone, the equal protection argument that the system discriminates against the poor largely falls away, and the Court declined to decide on injunctive relief.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred, noting some discomfort at the Court's reliance on scientific data but not exceeding judicial notice, saying he would allow a state to require certification by two physicians but not the complex Georgia scheme, and stating that the Court rejects any claim of a constitutional right to abortion on demand. Justice Douglas concurred, discussing the constitutional sources of a right of privacy, including liberty to make basic decisions about procreation and care of one's health, and arguing that the statute is overbroad, that the state may regulate abortion as a medical matter only through standards for physicians, and that the multiple-physician approval system destroys the privacy of the physician-patient relationship. Justice White, joined by Justice Rehnquist, dissented, finding no support in the Constitution's text or history for a right to abortion for reasons of convenience and arguing that the issue belongs to the people and legislatures; he would reverse the District Court. Justice Rehnquist also dissented separately, objecting to the compelling-state-interest test used to examine the Georgia provisions.","disposition":"The District Court's judgment was modified to strike the accreditation, committee approval, two-physician confirmation and residence requirements, and as modified was affirmed. Justice Blackmun wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Douglas, Brennan, Stewart, Marshall and Powell.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep410/usrep410179/usrep410179.pdf"},"1975/74-1303":{"facts":"Bishop was hired in 1969 as a probationary policeman for the city of Marion, North Carolina, and became a permanent employee after six months. In 1972 the City Manager, on the Chief of Police's recommendation, dismissed him without a hearing, telling him privately that the reasons were failure to follow orders, poor attendance at training classes, causing low morale, and conduct unsuited to an officer. A city ordinance said a permanent employee who failed to meet the standards of his classification, or was negligent, inefficient or unfit, could be dismissed by the City Manager, and that a discharged employee would be given written reasons on request. Bishop and other officers filed affidavits denying the charges.","procedure":"Bishop sued under 42 U.S.C. § 1983 for reinstatement and backpay, claiming deprivation of property and liberty without due process. The District Court granted summary judgment for the defendants, concluding that under state law he held his position at the will and pleasure of the city. The Court of Appeals affirmed, the en banc court being equally divided, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did Bishop's status as a permanent employee give him a property interest in continued employment protected by the Fourteenth Amendment, and did a false explanation for his discharge, given privately, deprive him of liberty?","holding":"Under the lower courts' tenable reading of state law, Bishop held his job at the city's will and so had no property interest. Assuming the stated reasons were false, the private communication of them did not deprive him of liberty, because they were not made public and truth or falsity does not change the liberty analysis.","reasoning":"Property interests are created by independent sources such as state law, so whether the ordinance created entitlement is a question of North Carolina law. There was no authoritative state-court interpretation, and the Court said the District Court's reading, that dismissal required no hearing or review and the employee had only procedural rights that were observed, was tenable, had some support in a North Carolina Supreme Court decision, and was accepted by the Court of Appeals, so the Court would not independently re-examine it. On liberty, discharge of an at-will employee without public disclosure of reasons is like the nonretention in Board of Regents v. Roth. The private oral explanation was not published, and the later answers to interrogatories came in litigation after the injury and could not supply retroactive support. Even if the reasons were false, their truth affects only whether the decision was prudent, not whether liberty was impaired; the Court said the Due Process Clause is not a guarantee against incorrect or ill-advised personnel decisions and federal courts are not the forum for reviewing routine personnel decisions.","separate":"Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, dissented: the stigmatizing reasons were disclosed and would likely reach prospective employers, so Bishop should have a hearing to clear his name; he criticized Paul v. Davis; and he argued that federal law informs the definition of property, so the question should be what the employee could reasonably expect, requiring remand for fact development. Justice White, joined by Justices Brennan, Marshall and Blackmun, dissented: the ordinance conditions dismissal on cause, and the majority revived the plurality view in Arnett v. Kennedy, rejected by six Justices there, that the procedures attached to a statutory right define the process due. Justice Blackmun, joined by Justice Brennan, dissented, joining White and adding that the North Carolina decision relied on was not authoritative and the ordinance's for-cause standard created a protected expectation of continued employment.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Stevens delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Powell and Rehnquist (5-4).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep426/usrep426341/usrep426341.pdf"},"1971/70-27":{"facts":"A Florida prosecuting attorney brought a state court proceeding to close Mitchum's bookstore as a public nuisance, and the state court entered a preliminary order barring the store's operation. After further inconclusive state proceedings, Mitchum sued in federal court. He alleged that state judicial and law enforcement officials, by applying Florida law unconstitutionally, were depriving him of First and Fourteenth Amendment rights and causing irreparable harm.","procedure":"Relying on 42 U.S.C. § 1983, Mitchum sought injunctive and declaratory relief against the state court proceeding. A three-judge District Court dissolved the temporary restraining orders and refused to enjoin, holding that the relief was not within any exception to the federal anti-injunction statute, 28 U.S.C. § 2283, because it was not expressly authorized by Act of Congress. Mitchum appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"Is 42 U.S.C. § 1983 an Act of Congress that 'expressly authorizes' an exception to the anti-injunction statute, so that a federal court in a § 1983 suit may enjoin a pending state court proceeding?","holding":"Section 1983 falls within the 'expressly authorized' exception of 28 U.S.C. § 2283, so the anti-injunction statute is not an absolute bar to a federal injunction against state court proceedings in a § 1983 action. The Court did not question the principles of equity, comity and federalism that restrain federal courts in such cases.","reasoning":"Under Atlantic Coast Line, the anti-injunction statute is an absolute ban unless a statutory exception applies, so the question was critical, and a contrary answer would also have undercut Younger v. Harris and its companion cases. Because Congress in 1948 restored the law as understood before Toucey, the Court applied its earlier criteria: an authorizing statute need not refer to § 2283 or expressly mention injunctions against state courts, but must create a specific, uniquely federal right or remedy, enforceable in a federal court of equity, that could be given its intended scope only by staying a state proceeding. Section 1983 was enacted in 1871 to enforce the Fourteenth Amendment against state action, including judicial action, and the debates show Congress knew state courts were failing to protect federal rights. Its purpose was to place the federal courts between the States and the people as guardians of federal rights, and it expressly authorizes a 'suit in equity.' The Court therefore held that § 1983 qualifies, while leaving intact the restraints of equity, comity and federalism.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger, joined by Justices White and Blackmun, concurred. He stressed that the Court held only that § 2283 does not bar such an injunction, and said that on remand the District Court should consider whether equity or federalism principles like those in Younger v. Harris prevent an injunction against the state nuisance-abatement proceedings, since the restraint applicable to civil proceedings had not been decided.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for further proceedings. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by all participating Justices; Justices Powell and Rehnquist took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep407/usrep407225/usrep407225.pdf"},"1974/73-1908":{"facts":"In 1972, Bethlehem Steel Corporation paid from general corporate funds for advertisements and mailings built around a speech by its chairman, Stewart Cort, that responded to a presidential candidate's statements about corporate taxes and urged \"truth squads\" in the campaign. Respondent Ash, who owned 50 shares of Bethlehem stock, sued Bethlehem's directors, claiming the spending violated 18 U.S.C. § 610, a criminal statute barring corporate contributions or expenditures in connection with federal elections. He sought an injunction against future spending and damages on behalf of the corporation.","procedure":"The District Court denied a preliminary injunction, and after Ash dropped his pendent Delaware-law claim rather than post security, it granted summary judgment to the defendants. The Third Circuit reversed, holding that a private cause of action under § 610 was proper for a citizen seeking an injunction or a stockholder seeking injunctive or derivative damage relief. The Supreme Court granted certiorari; meanwhile Congress had enacted the 1974 Federal Election Campaign Act Amendments creating the Federal Election Commission.","question":"Is a private cause of action, for injunction or derivative damages, to be implied in favor of a corporate stockholder under 18 U.S.C. § 610, a criminal statute that provides no civil remedy?","holding":"No. The 1974 Amendments, an intervening law, send complaints seeking an injunction against future § 610 violations to the Federal Election Commission, and a stockholder's derivative suit for damages for the 1972 violation cannot be implied from § 610 itself, leaving any remedy to Delaware corporate law.","reasoning":"A court must apply the law in effect when it decides, and the Amendments gave the Commission primary jurisdiction over complaints of § 610 violations and a route to injunctive relief through the Attorney General, so a private complainant must use that remedy for future elections. On damages, the Court set out four factors: whether the plaintiff is one of the class for whose especial benefit the statute was enacted, whether there is legislative intent to create or deny a remedy, whether a remedy is consistent with the statute's purposes, and whether the cause of action is one traditionally relegated to state law. Protecting stockholders was at best a secondary purpose of § 610, which aimed mainly at the corrupting influence of corporate wealth on elections. Nothing in the legislative history suggested a federal damages right, and repayment to the corporation would not undo or likely deter the initial misuse of funds. Corporate internal affairs are traditionally governed by state law, which may supply an ultra vires or fiduciary duty claim.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Brennan wrote the opinion for a unanimous Court.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep422/usrep422066/usrep422066.pdf"},"1971/70-5039":{"facts":"Margarita Fuentes bought a gas stove and a stereo from Firestone under conditional sales contracts and paid for over a year. After a dispute over servicing of the stove, Firestone sued for repossession and, on the same day and before she had been served, obtained a writ of replevin from a court clerk on a form and bond; a deputy sheriff then seized the goods from her home. In the companion Pennsylvania case, other buyers and one mother in a custody dispute with her ex-husband, a deputy sheriff, had household goods seized under similar writs obtained ex parte on the posting of a bond, in some cases without any court action ever being filed by the seizing party.","procedure":"The plaintiffs sued in federal court under 42 U.S.C. § 1983, challenging the Florida and Pennsylvania prejudgment replevin statutes under the Fourteenth Amendment's Due Process Clause. Three-judge District Courts in both cases upheld the statutes, and the Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction on appeal.","question":"Do state replevin statutes that let a private party obtain a writ for seizure of goods ex parte, on posting a bond and without notice or a prior hearing, deprive the possessor of property without due process of law?","holding":"Yes. The Florida and Pennsylvania prejudgment replevin provisions violate the Fourteenth Amendment insofar as they deny the possessor a prior opportunity to be heard before chattels are taken. The holding is narrow and leaves States free to design the form of prior hearing.","reasoning":"The central meaning of procedural due process is that parties whose rights are affected are entitled to be heard at a meaningful time, which here means before the seizure, when the deprivation can still be prevented rather than undone by a later hearing or damages. A bond and the applicant's conclusory allegation test only the applicant's own confidence and are no substitute for a neutral official's evaluation. The deprivation counts even though it may be temporary, even though the buyers lacked full title, and even though the household goods were not necessities, because any significant possessory interest is protected. Prior hearings may be postponed only in extraordinary situations involving an important governmental or public interest, a special need for prompt action, and strict state control through a government official under a narrowly drawn statute, and these statutes serve only private gain and leave the State acting largely in the dark. The fine-print repossession clauses were not a waiver, since they did not mention a prior hearing or the process of repossession and were part of a form contract.","separate":"Justice White, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justice Blackmun, dissented. He would have vacated and remanded for reconsideration under Younger v. Harris because state replevin proceedings were pending. On the merits, he argued that creditors have a protectable property interest too, that a mistaken claim of default is unlikely, that the decision calls into question the secured-transactions laws of nearly every State, and that creditors could avoid its effect through contract terms or short notice and a probable-cause hearing.","disposition":"Judgments of the District Courts vacated and cases remanded. Justice Stewart wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Douglas, Brennan and Marshall; Justices Powell and Rehnquist took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep407/usrep407067/usrep407067.pdf"},"1974/73-762":{"facts":"Carol Sosna moved from New York to Iowa in August 1972 with her three children and in September petitioned an Iowa court for dissolution of her marriage. Her husband, who remained in New York, was served when he visited Iowa and made a special appearance to contest jurisdiction. Iowa Code § 598.6 required a petitioner to have been \"for the last year a resident of the state,\" and the Iowa court dismissed her petition because she had not met that requirement.","procedure":"Rather than appeal in the Iowa courts, Sosna sued in the Northern District of Iowa under Rule 23 on behalf of a class of Iowa residents of under a year who wished to start divorce actions, seeking injunctive and declaratory relief against the State and the state judge. The parties stipulated to class treatment, the court approved it, and a three-judge court upheld the statute. By the time of appeal she had long satisfied the one-year period and had obtained a divorce in New York; the Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction and asked the parties to address Younger v. Harris.","question":"Is a class action challenging a state durational residency requirement for divorce moot once the named plaintiff has satisfied the requirement, and does a one-year residency requirement for divorce violate equal protection or due process?","holding":"The case is not moot, because after certification the class acquired a legal status separate from the named plaintiff and the controversy remains live for its unnamed members. On the merits, Iowa's one-year residency requirement for divorce is constitutional.","reasoning":"Had Sosna sued only for herself, the case would be moot, but the Court held that after certification \"the class of unnamed persons described in the certification acquired a legal status separate from the interest asserted by appellant.\" As in Dunn v. Blumstein, the state would keep enforcing the statute against class members although no single challenger would stay subject to it long enough to finish the litigation, and the named plaintiff had been a class member with a real and immediate injury at certification. The Court also found that Rule 23(a) adequacy of representation was met. On the merits, domestic relations are traditionally a state province, and 48 States impose some durational requirement. Shapiro, Dunn and Maricopa County were distinguished because those plaintiffs were irretrievably denied benefits, while Sosna faced only delay, and because Iowa's justification was not mere budget or administrative convenience. Iowa may require genuine attachment given the stakes for both spouses and any children, may avoid becoming a divorce mill, and may reduce the risk of collateral attack in other States under full faith and credit. No due process violation arose, since individualized proof of domicile would not itself have entitled her to a divorce under a one-year rule, and unlike Boddie there was no total denial of access.","separate":"Justice White dissented, arguing that the case was moot: Article III requires a named plaintiff with a live personal stake, certification does not substitute for one, and the Court should at least have remanded to consider mootness and adequacy of representation. Justice Marshall, joined by Justice Brennan, dissented on the merits, contending that the right to seek divorce is fundamental so that penalizing recent interstate migrants requires a compelling interest, and that a domicile requirement would protect Iowa's interests adequately.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Douglas, Stewart, Blackmun and Powell.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep419/usrep419393/usrep419393.pdf"},"1973/72-5847":{"facts":"Harrell Alexander, a Black worker, was fired from his job as a drill operator at Gardner-Denver in September 1969 for producing too many defective parts. He grieved the discharge under the collective-bargaining agreement, which barred discrimination and just-cause discharge and provided for binding arbitration, and raised a race discrimination claim at the last pre-arbitration step. Before the arbitration hearing he filed a charge with the Colorado Civil Rights Commission, which referred it to the EEOC. The arbitrator ruled the discharge was for just cause without mentioning discrimination, and the EEOC later found no reasonable cause.","procedure":"After receiving a right-to-sue notice, Alexander sued under Title VII in the District of Colorado. The district court granted summary judgment, holding that by electing to arbitrate he was bound by the arbitral decision and barred from suing. The Tenth Circuit affirmed per curiam, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Is an employee's right to a trial de novo under Title VII foreclosed by prior submission of the claim to final arbitration under the nondiscrimination clause of a collective-bargaining agreement?","holding":"No. An employee's statutory right to a trial de novo under Title VII is not foreclosed by prior submission of the claim to arbitration under a collective-bargaining agreement. The federal court may admit the arbitral decision as evidence and give it appropriate weight.","reasoning":"Title VII gives federal courts final enforcement responsibility, treats the private suit as essential, and was meant to supplement rather than supplant other remedies, so nothing in the statute makes an arbitral decision preclusive. Election of remedies does not apply, because the contractual right to arbitrate and the statutory right to equal employment opportunity are distinct even if one event violates both. There can be no prospective waiver of Title VII rights by a union, and merely resorting to arbitration is not a waiver. An arbitrator's authority is confined to the collective-bargaining agreement and does not extend to enforcing statutory rights, so he has \"no general authority to invoke public laws that conflict with the bargain between the parties.\" The employer does not have \"two strings to his bow\" inequity argument because Title VII gives employers no cause of action, and employers will still value arbitration for the no-strike pledge. A deferral rule would also be inappropriate because arbitration is less suited to Title VII issues: arbitrators know the law of the shop, factfinding is less complete, and the union controls the grievance. Courts may weigh the arbitral award according to the circumstances, including procedural fairness and the adequacy of the record.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Powell delivered the opinion for a unanimous Court.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep415/usrep415036/usrep415036.pdf"},"1978/78-690":{"facts":"Reiter sued on behalf of herself and all people in the United States who bought hearing aids made by five manufacturers. She alleged that the manufacturers committed antitrust violations, including vertical and horizontal price fixing and restrictions on their retail dealers, so that she and the class paid illegally inflated prices for hearing aids and related services. She sought treble damages under § 4 of the Clayton Act and injunctive relief under § 16. Section 4 allows suit by \"[a]ny person who shall be injured in his business or property\" by an antitrust violation.","procedure":"The manufacturers moved to dismiss, arguing that a retail consumer buying for personal use is not injured in her \"business or property.\" The District Court held that a purchaser is injured in property if violations raised the price paid, and certified the question for interlocutory appeal. The Eighth Circuit reversed, holding that consumers alleging no injury of a commercial or business nature lack standing under § 4, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Do consumers who pay a higher price for goods bought for personal use because of antitrust violations suffer an injury to their \"business or property\" within the meaning of § 4 of the Clayton Act?","holding":"Yes. A consumer whose money has been diminished by reason of an antitrust violation is injured in her \"property\" under § 4, even if she bought for personal use and lost only money.","reasoning":"The Court began with the statutory text. The word property naturally has a broad meaning covering anything of material value, and money is property. The disjunctive or shows that business was not meant to modify property, and the respondents' reading would have turned business into an adjective and stripped property of its independent meaning. The phrase still keeps restrictive force, for example by excluding personal injuries. Chattanooga Foundry held that a city paying inflated prices for pipe was injured in its property, so monetary injury alone can qualify. Consumer standing was implicit in Goldfarb and in many other cases referring to consumers, and Hawaii v. Standard Oil, with its language about commercial interests, did not hold otherwise. The legislative history, though not specific, shows the treble-damages remedy was meant to protect consumers from overcharges, and nobody questioned a consumer's right to sue. Concerns about crowded dockets and burdens on small businesses are policy matters for Congress, because \"We must take the statute as we find it,\" and district courts can use Rule 23 to screen frivolous or cumbersome class actions.","separate":"Justice Rehnquist concurred, joining the opinion but adding that the Court of Appeals' concern about added litigation and unfair settlements is well founded, that exhortations to district courts are not a complete solution, and that it is doubtful consumers rather than class attorneys benefit most; any problem is for Congress.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Chief Justice Burger delivered the opinion of the Court, in which all other Members joined except Justice Brennan, who took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep442/usrep442330/usrep442330.pdf"},"1975/74-1042":{"facts":"Ernst & Ernst, an accounting firm, audited the books of First Securities Company of Chicago, a small brokerage, from 1946 through 1967 and prepared reports the firm had to file with the SEC and the Midwest Stock Exchange. Respondents, customers of First Securities, invested in fictitious \"escrow\" accounts promoted by Leston Nay, the firm's president and 92% owner, who converted the money to his own use. The fraud surfaced in 1968 when Nay committed suicide and left a note calling the accounts spurious. Respondents claimed Ernst & Ernst negligently failed to audit properly and so missed Nay's rule that only he could open mail addressed to him, and they expressly disclaimed any allegation of fraud or intentional misconduct by the firm.","procedure":"Respondents sued Ernst & Ernst in the Northern District of Illinois under section 10(b) of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 and SEC Rule 10b-5, alleging that it aided and abetted Nay's fraud. The District Court granted summary judgment for Ernst & Ernst, finding no genuine issue about whether its audits met generally accepted standards. The Seventh Circuit reversed, holding that a party who breaches a duty of inquiry and disclosure is liable as an aider and abettor if the fraud would have been discovered or prevented but for the breach.","question":"Does a private action for damages lie under section 10(b) and Rule 10b-5 when the defendant is not alleged to have acted with scienter, that is, with intent to deceive, manipulate, or defraud?","holding":"No. A private damages action under section 10(b) and Rule 10b-5 requires an allegation of scienter; negligence alone is not enough. The Court did not decide whether recklessness suffices or whether scienter is required in an SEC injunctive action.","reasoning":"The Court began with the statutory text. The words \"manipulative or deceptive device or contrivance,\" and especially \"manipulative,\" which is nearly a term of art in securities markets, point to intentional or willful conduct rather than negligence. It rejected the SEC's effects-based argument, noting that the same logic would impose liability for wholly faultless conduct, and held that the Acts' remedial purpose does not justify departing from the statutory words. The legislative history, though silent on the point explicitly, shows no intent to reach negligent conduct. The statement of a drafters' spokesman described the provision as a catchall against new manipulative devices, and the committee reports tied liability to bad faith. The structure of the 1933 and 1934 Acts also pointed the same way. Where Congress created express civil liability it specified the required state of mind, and the express negligence-based remedies carry procedural limits (costs bonds, short limitation periods) that would be nullified if the same claims could proceed under section 10(b). Rule 10b-5 could not be read more broadly, because its administrative history shows it was aimed at fraud and because an agency's rulemaking power cannot exceed the statute that grants it. Remand was refused because respondents had proceeded throughout on a negligence theory and disclaimed intentional misconduct.","separate":"Justice Blackmun, joined by Justice Brennan, dissented. He argued that the language of subsections (b) and (c) of Rule 10b-5 is broad enough to reach negligent omissions and misstatements, that securities laws should be construed flexibly to serve their remedial purposes, and that the SEC and the Solicitor General took the same view. He saw no principled reason why negligence would violate the Rule in an SEC suit but not in a private suit, and he stressed the importance of the auditor's role. Justice Stevens took no part.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Powell wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White, Marshall, and Rehnquist (6-2, with Justice Stevens not participating).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep425/usrep425185/usrep425185.pdf"},"1974/73-1543":{"facts":"Willie Johnson, Jr., a Black employee of Railway Express Agency in Memphis, filed a timely charge with the EEOC on May 31, 1967, alleging race discrimination in seniority rules and job assignments, and later added a claim that he was fired on June 20, 1967 because of his race. The EEOC issued an investigation report in December 1967 but did not find reasonable cause until March 1970 and did not issue a right-to-sue notice until January 1971. Johnson then filed in federal court in 1971, asserting claims under Title VII and 42 U.S.C. section 1981, more than three and a half years after his cause of action accrued.","procedure":"The District Court dismissed the section 1981 claims as barred by Tennessee's one-year statute of limitations and dismissed the remaining claims on other grounds. The Sixth Circuit affirmed, rejecting the argument that the limitation period was suspended while the EEOC charge was pending. The Supreme Court granted certiorari limited to the limitation question.","question":"Does the timely filing of a Title VII charge with the EEOC toll the state limitation period applicable to a later action under 42 U.S.C. section 1981 based on the same facts?","holding":"No. Title VII and section 1981 provide separate, independent remedies, and filing an EEOC charge does not toll the borrowed state limitation period for a section 1981 action. Johnson's section 1981 suit was therefore time barred.","reasoning":"The Court first held that section 1981 reaches private employment discrimination and that Congress intended it to coexist with Title VII, so the remedies are \"separate, distinct, and independent,\" and a Title VII charge is not a prerequisite to a section 1981 suit. Because section 1981 has no federal limitation period, the Court borrowed the most appropriate state period, here Tennessee's one-year statute, and held that the state's tolling rules go with it, since the length of a period reflects a value judgment tied to its exceptions. Borrowed state law yields only if inconsistent with federal policy. The Court acknowledged that failing to toll may push claimants into court before EEOC conciliation ends, but answered that a plaintiff can ask for a stay and that Congress kept section 1981 independent, so the plaintiff could have sued at any time and in effect \"slept on his § 1981 rights.\" American Pipe and Burnett were distinguished because they involved federal limitation periods, a body of federal procedural law and conflicting federal policy, and prior filings of the identical cause of action.","separate":"Justice Marshall, joined by Justices Douglas and Brennan, concurred in Parts I-III (the independence of the two remedies) but dissented from Parts IV and V. He argued that federal courts apply federal equity principles to the effect of a borrowed limitation period, that federal policy favoring EEOC conciliation weighs strongly in favor of tolling, and that tolling would not revive stale claims because the respondents had notice through the EEOC charge. He would have reversed on the tolling point.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Blackmun wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White, Powell, and Rehnquist in full, and by Justices Douglas, Brennan, and Marshall as to Parts I-III.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep421/usrep421454/usrep421454.pdf"},"1972/71-1369":{"facts":"Respondents Rodriguez, Katzoff, and Kritsky were New York state prisoners in a conditional-release program under which they earned good-behavior credits toward their maximum sentences. Prison officials canceled credits through disciplinary proceedings: 120 days for Rodriguez (possession of contraband), 50 days for Katzoff (derogatory diary entries), and 545 days for Kritsky (alleged leadership in a prison protest, imposed summarily). Each alleged the cancellation violated the Constitution and sought restoration of the credits, which would have entitled him to immediate release on parole.","procedure":"Each respondent filed a civil rights action under 42 U.S.C. section 1983 joined with a habeas corpus petition. The District Courts treated the habeas claim as an adjunct, so no exhaustion of state remedies was required, and ruled for the prisoners on the merits. The Second Circuit, sitting en banc, affirmed, relying on Wilwording v. Swenson, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May a state prisoner who challenges the fact or duration of his confinement, by attacking the cancellation of good-conduct credits, obtain restoration of the credits through a section 1983 action without first exhausting state remedies, or is federal habeas corpus his exclusive federal remedy?","holding":"When a state prisoner challenges the very fact or duration of his physical imprisonment and seeks immediate or speedier release, his sole federal remedy is a writ of habeas corpus, with its exhaustion requirement. Section 1983 remains available for challenges to prison conditions and for damages claims.","reasoning":"The Court reasoned that \"the essence of habeas corpus is an attack by a person in custody upon the legality of that custody,\" and that respondents' claims fell within that traditional core even if restoration of credits would only shorten, rather than end, their confinement. Although section 1983 literally covers such claims, the more specific habeas statute, which Congress amended in 1948 to require exhaustion of adequate state remedies, controls over the general one, as respondents conceded it does for challenges to a conviction itself. The Court rejected the argument that exhaustion rests only on respect for state courts, finding comity equally implicated in state prison administration, which the States have a strong interest in correcting first. It addressed respondents' concerns about damages (a damages claim may proceed under section 1983 without exhaustion), delay (federal habeas is summary, while a section 1983 suit is a plenary action), and res judicata. Earlier cases such as Cooper, Houghton, Wilwording, and Haines were distinguished because those prisoners challenged conditions, not the fact or length of custody, and were reaffirmed on that understanding.","separate":"Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Douglas and Marshall, dissented. He argued that Wilwording controlled and that the Court had invented an unworkable \"core of habeas corpus\" distinction that would produce anomalies, such as split proceedings in state and federal court when a prisoner challenges both conditions and good-time credits. He contended that the no-exhaustion rule is an integral part of section 1983, that exhaustion in habeas protects relations between state and federal courts rather than state administrative action, and that nothing showed Congress meant habeas to displace section 1983 here.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Stewart wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices White, Blackmun, Powell, and Rehnquist (6-3).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep411/usrep411475/usrep411475.pdf"},"1970/36":{"facts":"Fred Mackey was convicted in January 1964 of five counts of income tax evasion for 1956 through 1960, in violation of 26 U.S.C. section 7201. The Government proved evasion by the net-worth method and introduced 60 monthly wagering excise tax returns that Mackey, who ran a policy wheel, had filed as the statute required, plus a summary exhibit and testimony that reported gross wagers less expenses exceeded the gambling profits on his income tax returns. His counsel objected that the returns were prejudicial, inflammatory, and irrelevant, but not on Fifth Amendment grounds. The Seventh Circuit affirmed in 1965. In January 1968 the Court decided Marchetti and Grosso, holding that the Fifth Amendment privilege was a defense to prosecution for failing to register as a gambler and pay the wagering taxes.","procedure":"After Marchetti and Grosso, Mackey, who was serving his sentence, moved under 28 U.S.C. section 2255 to vacate it, arguing the Fifth Amendment barred use of the wagering tax returns at his trial. The District Court denied the motion, and the Seventh Circuit affirmed, holding that Marchetti and Grosso would not be applied retroactively to upset a conviction obtained under the then-governing law.","question":"Do the Fifth Amendment holdings of Marchetti and Grosso apply retroactively to invalidate, on collateral review, a conviction at which compelled wagering tax returns were used as evidence of unreported income in a tax evasion prosecution?","holding":"No, the judgment denying relief was affirmed, but there was no majority rationale. A four-Justice plurality held that Marchetti and Grosso are not retroactive because they raise no doubt about the reliability of the verdict. Justice Harlan would apply the law in effect when the conviction became final on collateral review, and Justices Brennan and Marshall concluded that the Fifth Amendment does not bar use of the returns in a tax evasion prosecution.","reasoning":"The plurality (Justice White) relied on the Court's retroactivity line from Linkletter through Desist, and on Tehan v. Shott and Johnson v. New Jersey, which declined to apply new Fifth Amendment rules retroactively. Marchetti and Grosso broadened the privilege's reach without being motivated by concern that wagering returns were unreliable, and Mackey did not claim his returns were inaccurate, so the returns resembled reliable physical evidence seized under a later-changed Fourth Amendment rule. Because the old rule had been expressly approved in Kahriger and Lewis and the new one did not undermine the accuracy of the verdict, prospective application was enough. Justice Harlan, in a separate opinion that also addressed the companion Williams and Elkanich cases, rejected the Court's retroactivity approach as legislative in character. He would apply new rules to all cases on direct review but, on habeas, generally apply the law in force when the conviction became final, with exceptions for new substantive due process rules and for procedures implicit in ordered liberty. Applying that approach, he could not say that reliance on the pre-Marchetti rule worked an inexcusable inequity against Mackey.","separate":"Justice Harlan concurred in the judgment in this case in an opinion that also covered Nos. 81 and 82 (Williams and Elkanich). He argued that retroactivity on direct review must follow ordinary judicial principles, that on collateral review it concerns the scope and purposes of habeas corpus and finality favors applying the law as it stood when the conviction became final, with exceptions for substantive due process rules and procedures implicit in ordered liberty. He wrote that \"We sit as a court of law, not a council of revision.\" Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, concurred in the judgment on a different ground: the Government may enforce the tax laws through compelled reporting of income, so the Fifth Amendment protects against use of the information to prosecute gambling offenses but not against its use in a tax evasion prosecution. Justice Douglas, joined by Justice Black, dissented, saying he found nothing in the Constitution allowing some constitutional rules to be retroactive and others only prospective, and that Mackey should be treated like the defendants in Marchetti and Grosso.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice White announced the judgment of the Court and wrote an opinion joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart and Blackmun; Justices Harlan, Brennan, and Marshall concurred in the judgment in separate opinions, and Justices Douglas and Black dissented (7-2 on the judgment).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep401/usrep401667/usrep401667.pdf"},"1978/78-5384":{"facts":"In November 1976, 18-year-old David Sandstrom confessed to killing Annie Jessen. He was charged with deliberate homicide under a Montana statute, which required that he \"purposely or knowingly\" caused the death. At trial his lawyer conceded the killing but argued, relying on two court-appointed mental health experts, that because of a personality disorder aggravated by alcohol Sandstrom did not act purposely or knowingly and was guilty only of a lesser crime. Over defense objection, the judge instructed the jury that \"the law presumes that a person intends the ordinary consequences of his voluntary acts.\"","procedure":"The jury convicted Sandstrom of deliberate homicide and he was sentenced to 100 years. The Montana Supreme Court affirmed, accepting that a presumption may not shift the burden of proof but holding that the instruction placed on the defendant only a burden to produce some evidence, and so did not violate due process. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"In a case where intent is an element of the crime, does a jury instruction that \"the law presumes that a person intends the ordinary consequences of his voluntary acts\" violate the Fourteenth Amendment's requirement that the State prove every element of the offense beyond a reasonable doubt?","holding":"Yes. A reasonable juror could have read the instruction as either a conclusive presumption or one shifting the burden of persuasion on intent, and either reading would relieve the State of its burden under In re Winship, so the instruction is unconstitutional.","reasoning":"The Court held that the effect of a presumption depends on how a reasonable juror could have understood it, not on the state court's view of its legal import. The jury was told only that the law presumes intent, without being told it could infer intent or that the presumption could be rebutted, so it could readily have taken the instruction as mandatory. The Court rejected the State's characterizations of the instruction as a permissive inference or as a mere burden of production. A juror could have treated the presumption as conclusive, which would conflict with the presumption of innocence and invade the jury's factfinding role as in Morissette and United States Gypsum. Alternatively, the juror could have read it as shifting the burden of persuasion, which Mullaney v. Wilbur forbids; Montana's own evidence rules require a disputable presumption to be overcome by a preponderance of evidence. Because Winship holds that due process requires \"proof beyond a reasonable doubt of every fact necessary to constitute the crime,\" either reading was error. The State's argument that the jury might have convicted on knowledge alone failed because the verdict was general and the jury might have relied on the invalid instruction. The Court left harmless error to the Montana courts.","separate":"Justice Rehnquist, joined by Chief Justice Burger, concurred. He said he was reluctant for the Court to parse state jury instructions and noted that a permissive inference would raise no constitutional problem, but he deferred to the majority's view that the jury may have understood the instruction as mandatory, and that the difference between \"infer\" and \"presume\" may have mattered.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for further proceedings. Justice Brennan wrote the opinion for a unanimous Court.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep442/usrep442510/usrep442510.pdf"},"1974/73-1977":{"facts":"The Wilderness Society, Environmental Defense Fund and Friends of the Earth sued the Secretary of the Interior in 1970 to stop permits for the trans-Alaska oil pipeline, arguing that the right-of-way widths violated the Mineral Leasing Act of 1920 and that the Secretary had not complied with the National Environmental Policy Act. Alyeska Pipeline Service Co., the consortium that would build the pipeline, later intervened. The Court of Appeals ruled for the groups on the Mineral Leasing Act ground alone, and Congress then amended that Act to authorize the permits, ending the merits dispute. The groups claimed 4,455 hours of attorneys' time.","procedure":"The District Court had dissolved the preliminary injunction and dismissed the complaint, but the en banc D.C. Circuit reversed on the Mineral Leasing Act ground. After Congress changed the law, the en banc Court of Appeals awarded respondents attorneys' fees against Alyeska, for half the full award, under the “private attorney general” theory, and remanded for the amount; it had rejected the bad-faith and common-benefit exceptions as inapplicable. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May a federal court, in the exercise of its equitable powers and without statutory authorization, award attorneys' fees to a prevailing party that acted as a “private attorney general” vindicating important public policies?","holding":"No. Under the American Rule, the prevailing litigant ordinarily cannot recover attorneys' fees absent statute or enforceable contract, and the federal courts may not create a private-attorney-general exception; any such departure must come from Congress.","reasoning":"The Court traced the history of fee practice in federal courts: from early reliance on state practice, through Arcambel v. Wiseman, to the 1853 fee-bill act and its successors, now 28 U.S.C. sections 1920 and 1923, which limit the fees taxable as costs. The existing judicial exceptions (common fund or common benefit, willful disobedience of a court order, and bad faith) are recognized assertions of inherent equitable power, but none was relied on by the Court of Appeals. Congress has made specific fee-shifting provisions in many statutes, which differ in whether awards are mandatory or discretionary and who may recover, showing that the circumstances and range of discretion are matters for Congress. A judge-made rule would require courts to decide which statutes are important enough, and who may be awarded fees and against whom, without standards. It would also collide with 28 U.S.C. section 2412, which generally bars fee awards against the United States, so the rule would operate only against private parties. Congress's use of the private-attorney-general idea in particular statutes is not a grant of authority to the courts to adopt it generally. The Court said it would not “invade the legislature's province” by redistributing litigation costs as the Court of Appeals had.","separate":"Justice Brennan dissented in a short opinion, agreeing with Justice Marshall that federal equity courts have power to award fees on a private-attorney-general rationale and, for the reasons given by Judge Wright below, that this was a proper case. Justice Marshall dissented, arguing that the availability of fees rests on independent equitable power, not just statute, as shown by Sprague, Mills v. Electric Auto-Lite and Hall v. Cole, and that section 1923 is not a bar. He thought workable standards could be drawn from prior cases (an important right shared by the public, a plaintiff interest too small to justify counsel, and fees that would fall on a benefiting class), found them met here, and would have affirmed the half-fee award against Alyeska. Justices Douglas and Powell took no part.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice White wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Blackmun and Rehnquist; Justices Douglas and Powell took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep421/usrep421240/usrep421240.pdf"},"1974/74-214":{"facts":"Appellee Salfi married wage earner Londo Salfi in May 1972; he had a heart attack less than a month later and died in November 1972, less than six months after the marriage. She applied for mother's insurance benefits for herself and child's insurance benefits for her daughter by a previous marriage. The Social Security Administration denied both applications, initially and on reconsideration, solely because the Social Security Act defines “widow” and “child” to exclude surviving wives and stepchildren whose relationship to the deceased wage earner lasted less than nine months before his death.","procedure":"The appellees filed a class action in federal district court, invoking 28 U.S.C. section 1331 and seeking declaratory and injunctive relief and retroactive benefits. A three-judge District Court held that the nine-month requirements were unconstitutional irrebuttable presumptions under Stanley v. Illinois, Vlandis v. Kline and Cleveland Board of Education v. LaFleur, certified a class, and enjoined the denial of benefits on that basis. The government appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"Did the District Court have jurisdiction over the suit given 42 U.S.C. section 405(h), and, if so, are the Act's nine-month duration-of-relationship requirements for widows and stepchildren unconstitutional as conclusive presumptions?","holding":"Section 405(h) bars federal-question jurisdiction under section 1331, so jurisdiction existed only under section 405(g), and only over the named appellees, not unnamed class members who had not alleged any application or decision. On the merits, the nine-month requirements are constitutional because they are rationally related to the legitimate goal of preventing sham marriages entered to obtain benefits.","reasoning":"The third sentence of section 405(h) is sweeping and not a mere codification of exhaustion, and the claim, though raising a constitutional issue, also arises under the Act, which supplies standing and the substantive basis; Johnson v. Robison was distinguished because the statute there reached only decisions of the Administrator and would have left no judicial review. Section 405(g) supplied jurisdiction for the named appellees because the Secretary, by not challenging their allegations of exhaustion, in effect treated the reconsideration decision as final and a hearing would have been futile. On the merits, social welfare classifications need only be rationally based and free from invidious discrimination, and the Court said “a noncontractual claim to receive funds from the public treasury enjoys no constitutionally protected status.” Stanley, Vlandis and LaFleur were distinguished because the Act sets an objective eligibility criterion that claimants can try to meet rather than speaking of good faith and then excluding evidence of it. Congress had long been concerned about marriages entered to obtain benefits, and could rationally adopt an imprecise prophylactic rule, like a life insurance suicide clause, given the many claimants and the difficulty of reliably determining intent or knowledge of terminal illness through individual hearings.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented, agreeing with Justice Brennan that jurisdiction was clear and the Court's discussion unwarranted; on the merits he objected to legislatively created presumptions as invading the right to jury trial, and would have remanded to let Mrs. Salfi show her marriage was not a sham. Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, dissented. He argued that the jurisdictional question had not been briefed, that section 405(h) only codifies exhaustion and does not reach a claim arising under the Constitution or a suit for declaratory and injunctive relief (relying on Johnson v. Robison), that the Secretary had not actually waived exhaustion, and that on the merits the conclusive presumption was irrational and contrary to Vlandis, Murry and Jimenez, so that individualized proof should have been allowed.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White, Blackmun and Powell.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep422/usrep422749/usrep422749.pdf"},"1970/5586":{"facts":"Bell, a clergyman who traveled by car to serve three rural Georgia communities, was involved in a 1968 accident when a five-year-old girl rode her bicycle into the side of his car. Her parents reported injuries and claimed $5,000 in damages. Under Georgia's Motor Vehicle Safety Responsibility Act, the Director of Public Safety told Bell that, since he was uninsured, he must post $5,000 in security or have his license and registration suspended. At the administrative hearing the Director refused to consider Bell's evidence that he was not at fault, because the only matters the Department could consider were involvement in the accident, compliance with the Act, and statutory exceptions.","procedure":"Bell appealed de novo to the Superior Court, which heard his evidence, found him free from fault, and ordered that his license not be suspended pending a damages suit. The Georgia Court of Appeals reversed, holding fault and innocence completely irrelevant under the Act and rejecting his due process claim; the Georgia Supreme Court denied review and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the Fourteenth Amendment's Due Process Clause allow Georgia to suspend an uninsured motorist's driver's license and registration after an accident, without a prior hearing on whether he was at fault or liable?","holding":"No. Because the licenses are important interests that cannot be taken without procedural due process, and because the Georgia scheme makes liability a significant factor, the State must, before suspension, provide a forum to determine whether there is a reasonable possibility of a judgment being rendered against the licensee as a result of the accident.","reasoning":"Once issued, a driver's license may become essential to a livelihood, so its suspension is state action adjudicating important interests, and the same constraints apply whether the entitlement is called a right or a privilege, as in Goldberg v. Kelly and Sniadach. The required inquiry need not be a full adjudication of liability, which can occur only in litigation between the parties, but only a determination of a reasonable possibility of a judgment in the amounts claimed. The State's interest in security for claimants does not justify denying the hearing where no judgment is reasonably possible, and added expense does not outweigh due process. Georgia's argument that fault is irrelevant failed because, looking to substance, the Act lifts or avoids suspension on a release from liability or an adjudication of nonliability, so it is not a no-fault scheme. A hearing that excludes an element essential to the decision is not meaningful, and except in emergencies the hearing must come before the termination takes effect. Georgia may choose among several ways to comply.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger and Justices Black and Blackmun concurred in the result; the opinion states no separate reasons.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Brennan wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Douglas, Harlan, Stewart, White and Marshall; Chief Justice Burger and Justices Black and Blackmun concurred in the result.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep402/usrep402535/usrep402535.pdf"},"1972/72-634":{"facts":"The National Association of Letter Carriers, six individual federal employees, and local Democratic and Republican committees challenged section 9(a) of the Hatch Act, now 5 U.S.C. section 7324(a)(2), which bars federal employees from taking “an active part in political management or in political campaigns,” defined as the acts prohibited for competitive-service employees by Civil Service Commission determinations before July 19, 1940. The plaintiffs said they wanted to run for local offices, serve as convention delegates and party officers, campaign for candidates, work at polls, and write political letters, and that the Commission was enforcing or threatening to enforce the ban. One plaintiff had resigned from federal employment to run for state office and then faced delayed reemployment.","procedure":"A three-judge District Court for the District of Columbia held the provision unconstitutional on its face as vague and overbroad, reading United Public Workers v. Mitchell as leaving the definition open, and enjoined enforcement. The government appealed directly, and the Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction on the single question of facial invalidity.","question":"Is the Hatch Act's prohibition on federal employees taking an active part in political management or political campaigns unconstitutional on its face for vagueness or overbreadth under the First Amendment and Fifth Amendment?","holding":"No. Congress may constitutionally bar federal employees from plainly identifiable partisan political management and campaign activity, as Mitchell held, and the provision, read with the Civil Service Commission's regulations, is neither unconstitutionally vague nor substantially overbroad.","reasoning":"The Court reaffirmed Mitchell and described a century of history, from the Pendleton Act and Civil Service Rule I to the Hatch Act, in which the country judged that federal service should rest on merit rather than political service and that employee influence on elections should be limited. Applying the Pickering balance between employee speech and the government's interest as employer, the Court found the interests substantial: impartial execution of the laws, avoiding the appearance of political justice, preventing a government work force from being built into a political machine, and protecting employees from pressure. The restrictions are not aimed at any party or viewpoint. On vagueness, Congress's 1940 substitute for section 15 meant that the statute incorporates the Commission's pre-1940 adjudications as summarized in Form 1236 and refined in the 1970 regulations, and the Court said “our task is not to destroy the Act if we can, but to construe it.” The regulations list the prohibited acts in terms an ordinary person can understand, and the Commission offers advisory opinions. Any overbreadth in the restrictions on endorsements and speeches at party gatherings is not substantial and would not invalidate the whole statute.","separate":"Justice Douglas, joined by Justices Brennan and Marshall, dissented. He argued that the Act, which incorporates thousands of Commission rulings, lacks precision and chills First Amendment speech, petition and assembly by employees on their own time; that Mitchell's rational-basis approach is outdated in light of Pickering, Wood v. Georgia and Perry v. Sindermann; that the Commission's open-ended list of prohibited acts gives it unbridled discretion (illustrated by a ruling against a worker who placed a newspaper ad for his father's candidacy); and that the provision should be struck down so Congress could start anew.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice White wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Blackmun, Powell and Rehnquist; Justices Douglas, Brennan and Marshall dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep413/usrep413548/usrep413548.pdf"},"1969/301":{"facts":"Bruce Church, Inc. grew high-quality cantaloupes on about 6,400 leased acres at Parker, Arizona, in which it had invested over $3,000,000. Because it had no packing sheds there, and the perishable melons had to be packed immediately, it trucked them in bulk to its packing facilities at Blythe, California, 31 miles away, where they were packed and shipped in containers bearing the California packer's name. In 1968 Arizona's fruit and vegetable supervisor, enforcing the state Fruit and Vegetable Standardization Act, ordered that the cantaloupes not be shipped out of state unless packed in approved containers, which could be done only in packing sheds. The parties stipulated that compliance would require building packing facilities near Parker at a cost of about $200,000, and the company faced loss of a $700,000 crop.","procedure":"The company sued in federal court to enjoin the order. A three-judge District Court granted temporary relief and then a permanent injunction, holding that the order was an unlawful burden on interstate commerce. The state official appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"Does an Arizona order that requires cantaloupes grown in the State to be packed in Arizona before interstate shipment, in order to identify them as Arizona produce, unconstitutionally burden interstate commerce?","holding":"Yes. The order burdens interstate commerce, and Arizona's minimal interest in having the company's high-quality cantaloupes identified as Arizona-grown cannot justify forcing the company to build and operate an unneeded $200,000 packing plant in the State.","reasoning":"The order does burden interstate commerce, because the melons were headed to an ascertainable place in California and the order would require an operation done out of state to be done in state; Federal Compress and Chassaniol, involving taxes on goods at rest in a State, were distinguished. The Court stated the general rule that where a statute regulates evenhandedly to effectuate a legitimate local interest and has only incidental effects on interstate commerce, it will be upheld unless the burden is clearly excessive in relation to the local benefits, with the extent of tolerable burden depending on the nature of the local interest and on whether it could be promoted with less impact. The Act's purpose, protecting the reputation of Arizona growers against deceptive packaging, is legitimate, but applied here it served only to enhance Arizona's reputation through the company's superior produce, and the company was not misusing the State's name. The Court said “The nature of that burden is, constitutionally, more significant than its extent,” since requirements that business operations be performed in the home State have been viewed with particular suspicion (Foster-Fountain Packing, Toomer v. Witsell). The State's interest, assumed legitimate, was minimal and less than the interest in local employment, which the Commerce Clause also does not permit to justify such a requirement.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Stewart wrote the opinion of the Court; the opinion text does not state a vote count and notes no separate opinions.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep397/usrep397137/usrep397137.pdf"},"1976/75-1721":{"facts":"In May 1973 railroad officials in San Diego noticed that a heavy footlocker loaded by Machado and Leary was leaking talcum powder, and federal agents were alerted. When the train reached Boston, agents with a trained dog saw the dog signal the presence of a controlled substance in the footlocker. After Chadwick joined the others and the footlocker was placed in the trunk of his car, agents arrested all three. They took the footlocker to the Federal Building, where it stayed under their exclusive control, and opened it about an hour and a half after the arrests without consent or a warrant, finding large amounts of marihuana.","procedure":"Respondents were indicted for possession of marihuana with intent to distribute and conspiracy, and moved to suppress the marihuana. The District Court granted the motion, rejecting both the automobile exception and search incident to arrest, and a divided Court of Appeals for the First Circuit affirmed. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Is a search warrant required before federal agents may open a locked footlocker that they have lawfully seized at the time of its owners' arrest, when they have probable cause to believe it contains contraband?","holding":"Yes. Once the agents had the footlocker under their exclusive control and no exigency existed, the Warrant Clause required a warrant before the search, and the search was justified neither by the automobile exception nor as incident to arrest.","reasoning":"The Court rejected the Government's argument that the Warrant Clause protects only homes and similar places, since the Fourth Amendment “protects people, not places” and a warrant's neutral scrutiny is as valuable outside the home. By locking personal effects in a double-locked footlocker, respondents manifested an expectation of privacy that is due the Warrant Clause's protection. The automobile exception rests on the diminished privacy expectation in vehicles, which does not apply to luggage that serves as a repository of personal effects, and the footlocker's mobility did not justify dispensing with a warrant once it was immobilized under police control. A search incident to arrest is justified by the danger that the arrestee will use a weapon or destroy evidence, so it cannot cover luggage that is remote in time or place from the arrest or already in exclusive police control. The Court drew the line where the property comes under the exclusive dominion of police authority, even though a warrant would likely have been issued.","separate":"Justice Brennan concurred, joining the opinion in full, to criticize the Government's “extreme view” of the Fourth Amendment and to note that the dissent's alternative courses of action were supported only by court of appeals decisions and are not clearly lawful. Justice Blackmun, joined by Justice Rehnquist, dissented, agreeing the Warrant Clause is not limited to the home but arguing that police should be allowed to search without a warrant any movable property seized with a person properly arrested in a public place, that the majority's rule makes outcomes turn on fortuitous circumstances, and that other doctrines would often allow such searches anyway.","disposition":"Affirmed. Chief Justice Burger delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, Stewart, White, Marshall, Powell and Stevens (7-2).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep433/usrep433001/usrep433001.pdf"},"1973/72-953":{"facts":"Nineteen residents of Cairo, Illinois (17 black, two white) sued as a class, alleging that officials in Alexander County engaged in discriminatory criminal-justice practices to deter their civil-rights protests and boycott of merchants. As to the petitioners, a magistrate and a circuit judge, they alleged an unofficial bond schedule applied without regard to individual circumstances, harsher sentences and conditions for black residents, and charging for jury trials in city-ordinance cases. They sought only an injunction against these practices, not damages. The complaint described these practices in general terms and did not identify any named plaintiff as having suffered them.","procedure":"The District Court dismissed for want of jurisdiction to grant the injunction and on judicial immunity grounds. The Seventh Circuit reversed, holding that injunctions against judicial officers are not barred if they knowingly engage in intentional racial discrimination, and that the District Court should fashion appropriate relief if the allegations were proved. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does a complaint seeking to enjoin a magistrate's and judge's alleged discriminatory bond, sentencing, and jury-fee practices present an actual case or controversy under Article III, and does it state a basis for federal equitable relief against state criminal proceedings?","holding":"The complaint failed to allege an actual case or controversy, because none of the named plaintiffs was shown to have suffered or to face a real and immediate threat of injury from the challenged practices. Even if there were a controversy, the injunction sought would intrude on state criminal proceedings contrary to principles of equity, comity, and federalism, and the plaintiffs showed no irreparable injury or inadequate legal remedy.","reasoning":"Plaintiffs must allege a \"personal stake in the outcome,\" and a real and immediate injury rather than a conjectural one; if no named class representative meets that requirement, none may seek relief for the class. The complaint alleged injury only in general terms, and past exposure to illegal conduct does not show a present controversy for injunctive relief absent continuing effects. The prospect of future injury depended on respondents again being arrested, charged, and brought before petitioners, which would require speculation, especially since no unconstitutional statute was challenged and the Court assumed respondents would stay within the law. On the alternative ground, the requested injunction would amount to \"an ongoing federal audit of state criminal proceedings,\" requiring continuing federal supervision and contempt proceedings, in conflict with Younger v. Harris. The Court also found irreparable injury and inadequacy of legal remedies unshown, pointing to state remedies (change of judge, appeal, collateral review, judicial discipline), federal habeas, and criminal liability under 18 U.S.C. section 242.","separate":"Justice Blackmun concurred in the judgment and in Part I, but argued that once the Court found no case or controversy, its discussion of equitable relief in Part II was an advisory opinion it could not properly give. Justice Douglas, joined by Justices Brennan and Marshall, dissented, arguing that the complaint's allegations of a continuing pattern of discriminatory bail and sentencing were specific enough to state a case or controversy and warranted a trial, that remedies should be decided after trial, and that Part II was advisory if there was no controversy.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Powell, and Rehnquist; Justice Blackmun concurred in the judgment and in Part I; Justices Douglas, Brennan, and Marshall dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep414/usrep414488/usrep414488.pdf"},"1975/75-252":{"facts":"After nine serious fires over about two and a half months in 1974 at the medium-security Massachusetts Correctional Institution at Norfolk, six inmates were removed from the general population based mainly on informant reports. The prison's Classification Board held hearings, with counsel present, on whether to transfer them to other institutions, possibly maximum-security ones where conditions were substantially less favorable. The Board heard the superintendent's account of informant information outside the inmates' presence, and the inmates never received transcripts or summaries of it. The inmates could present their own evidence and denied involvement. The Board's recommendations, as reviewed and modified by the Commissioner of Corrections, resulted in transfers to Walpole and Bridgewater, none involving loss of good time or disciplinary confinement.","procedure":"The inmates sued under 42 U.S.C. section 1983 for an injunction, declaratory relief, and damages. The District Court, reading Wolff v. McDonnell, held the notice and hearing inadequate and ordered the inmates returned to the general population until transferred after proper procedures. A divided First Circuit affirmed, holding the transfers significantly changed confinement conditions and triggered due process protections. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment entitle a state prisoner to a factfinding hearing before transfer to a prison with substantially less favorable conditions, where state law does not condition transfers on specific misconduct or other events?","holding":"No. A transfer to a less favorable prison does not infringe or implicate a liberty interest within the Due Process Clause, either by its own force or through state law, where state officials have discretion to transfer prisoners for any reason or no reason, so no hearing is constitutionally required.","reasoning":"The Court said that not every grievous loss triggers due process; the nature of the interest, not its weight, controls. Given a valid conviction, the State may confine the prisoner in any of its institutions and subject him to its prison rules, so confinement in any state prison is within the range of custody the conviction authorized, even if one is far more disagreeable. Treating any substantial deprivation as requiring a hearing would subject a wide range of discretionary prison administration to federal judicial review. Wolff v. McDonnell was distinguished because the liberty interest there came from state law, which made good-time credits forfeitable only for serious misbehavior. Massachusetts law gave no right to remain in a particular prison and vested transfer authority in officials in many circumstances. That misconduct often prompts a transfer does not change this, and any expectation of staying put is \"too ephemeral and insubstantial to trigger procedural due process protections.\" States remain free to require hearings by statute or rule.","separate":"Justice Stevens, joined by Justices Brennan and Marshall, dissented. He objected most to the Court's view that liberty protected by the Due Process Clause comes only from the Constitution or state law, arguing instead that liberty is a basic freedom that persists in some measure during lawful custody. He would hold that a sufficiently grievous change in a prisoner's status may not be imposed arbitrarily, and agreed with the Court of Appeals that these transfers were serious enough to require due process.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Blackmun, Powell, and Rehnquist; Justices Stevens, Brennan, and Marshall dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep427/usrep427215/usrep427215.pdf"},"1969/153":{"facts":"Respondents Dash, Richardson and Williams were each convicted in a New York state court of a felony (robbery, murder, robbery) on a plea of guilty entered on the advice of counsel. Each later claimed in a habeas corpus petition that the plea was the product of a coerced confession. Dash also alleged that the trial judge threatened a 60-year sentence if he went to trial. Richardson alleged his assigned lawyer advised him to plead and raise the confession later by habeas corpus. Williams alleged his lawyer ignored his alibi and misdescribed the plea as to a misdemeanor.","procedure":"State courts denied collateral relief without hearings, and the federal District Courts denied the habeas petitions without hearings. The Second Circuit reversed in each case and ordered hearings, holding that a plea is not voluntary if it results from an involuntary confession, at least where entered in New York before Jackson v. Denno. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May a counseled, otherwise valid guilty plea be attacked in collateral proceedings, and a hearing obtained, merely by alleging that the plea was motivated by a prior coerced confession, including where the plea was entered before Jackson v. Denno?","holding":"A defendant who alleges that he pleaded guilty because of a prior coerced confession is not, without more, entitled to a hearing on a habeas corpus petition. A plea based on reasonably competent advice is an intelligent plea, and the pre-Jackson timing of the plea does not change that result.","reasoning":"The Court reasoned that a defendant who deems his confession inadmissible and would otherwise go to trial is, by pleading guilty, bypassing his chance to raise the confession claim in state court, and the more plausible explanation is that he predicted the confession would be admitted. Nothing in that sequence makes the plea itself involuntary; at most it shows a misjudgment of admissibility. Plea advice necessarily involves uncertain predictions, and the question is whether counsel's advice was within the range of competence demanded of attorneys in criminal cases, not whether it proved correct. As to Jackson v. Denno, a plea is judged by whether it was voluntary and intelligent under the law then existing, and counsel could not be faulted for relying on Stein v. New York or for not anticipating Jackson. A guilty plea rests on the defendant's own admission in open court, not on the confession, so Jackson's retroactivity for trial convictions does not extend to pleas. The Court expressed no disagreement with the Court of Appeals on the other claims (the judge's threat, inadequacy of counsel).","separate":"Justice Black concurred in the opinion and judgment while adhering to his separate opinion in Jackson v. Denno. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Douglas and Marshall, dissented. He argued that a plea induced by a coerced confession is invalid under Pennsylvania ex rel. Herman v. Claudy and Chambers v. Florida, that the presence of counsel should not be given talismanic effect, and that the plea should be treated as the fruit of the State's illegal conduct as in Harrison v. United States. Because New York's pre-Jackson procedure was constitutionally defective, counsel's advice could not cure it. He criticized the Court for making Jackson only partially retroactive and would have affirmed the order for hearings on the coerced-confession claims.","disposition":"The judgments of the Court of Appeals were vacated and the case remanded for further proceedings consistent with the opinion. Justice White wrote the opinion of the Court. Justice Black concurred; Justices Brennan, Douglas and Marshall dissented (the text does not state a vote count).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep397/usrep397759/usrep397759.pdf"},"1970/95":{"facts":"A Wisconsin statute let designated persons, including a wife, mayor, police chief, sheriff or district attorney, forbid in writing all sales or gifts of liquor to a person who by excessive drinking exposed himself or family to want or became dangerous to community peace. Selling or giving liquor to such a person was a misdemeanor. The Hartford police chief, without notice or hearing, had notices posted in all retail liquor outlets in Hartford forbidding sales or gifts of liquor to appellee, a Hartford resident, for one year.","procedure":"Appellee sued the chief for damages and injunctive relief in federal district court. Wisconsin intervened on the injunction issue, and a three-judge court, by a divided vote, held the Act unconstitutional on its face and enjoined its enforcement. The State appealed and the Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction.","question":"Does a state statute that permits an official to post a person as forbidden to buy liquor, without notice or a hearing, violate procedural due process? And should the federal court have abstained to let state courts construe the statute first?","holding":"Where a statute attaches a stigma to a person by official posting, procedural due process requires notice and an opportunity to be heard, and the Wisconsin Act, which provides none, is unconstitutional. Abstention was not required because the statute is unambiguous and presents no uncertain question of state law.","reasoning":"The Court acknowledged the State's broad police power over intoxicating liquors, so the only issue was whether the label imposed by posting is a stigma requiring procedural protection. Drawing on earlier decisions, it stated that where the State attaches a \"badge of infamy\" to a citizen, due process comes into play, and that where a person's good name or reputation is at stake because of government action, \"notice and an opportunity to be heard are essential.\" Posting may be a mark of illness to some but is an official branding to others, and the appellee had no chance to defend herself. On abstention, the doctrine applies only where the issue of state law is uncertain. Here the Act contains no provision for notice and hearing and is unambiguous, so the naked question was its facial constitutionality, and under Zwickler v. Koota the federal court should decide it rather than await state court proceedings.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger, joined by Justice Blackmun, dissented. He thought the result probably correct on the merits, but would have required the parties to resort to the state courts first, citing Reetz v. Bozanich and the abstention policy even for a plain statute, and noted that counsel had deliberately avoided state court to obtain a three-judge court and direct appeal. Justice Black, joined by Justice Blackmun, also dissented. He would have vacated and remanded so state courts could construe the Act, suggested that other state law might supply notice and hearing, and compared the Act's unbounded ex parte power to a bill of attainder.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Douglas delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Harlan, Brennan, Stewart, White and Marshall. Chief Justice Burger and Justice Blackmun dissented, as did Justice Black (joined by Justice Blackmun).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep400/usrep400433/usrep400433.pdf"},"1973/72-6476":{"facts":"Petitioners were recipients of Aid to Families with Dependent Children (AFDC), a cooperative federal-state program. Each had been unable to pay rent and, facing eviction, received emergency rent payments from the county social services department. New York characterized these payments as advances and, under a state regulation, deducted them from later monthly grants. Petitioners sued under 42 U.S.C. section 1983, alleging that the recoupment regulation violated the Equal Protection Clause and conflicted with the Social Security Act and HEW regulations.","procedure":"The District Court found the equal protection claim substantial, took pendent jurisdiction of the statutory claim, and declared the regulation invalid under the Social Security Act and HEW regulations and enjoined it. The Court of Appeals reversed, holding that because no substantial constitutional claim was presented, the District Court lacked jurisdiction over either claim. The Supreme Court granted certiorari on the jurisdictional question.","question":"Did petitioners' equal protection claim satisfy the substantiality requirement for jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. section 1343(3), and if so, could the District Court decide the pendent statutory claim of conflict with federal law before the constitutional claim?","holding":"The equal protection claim was not so insubstantial as to defeat jurisdiction, so the District Court had jurisdiction under section 1343(3). With that jurisdiction, it could also decide the pendent statutory claim first, without convening a three-judge court, and should not have dismissed it.","reasoning":"The Court accepted the rule that a federal claim must be substantial to support jurisdiction, but said it is insubstantial only if obviously frivolous or if prior decisions clearly foreclose it. Dandridge v. Williams applied rational-basis review to welfare regulations but did not suspend the Equal Protection Clause, and no decision of the Court addressed a regulation like this one, whose rationality was not immediately obvious. Under Bell v. Hood, a claim that may fail on the merits is not for that reason beyond jurisdiction. The statutory claim was properly decided first, because a nonconstitutional ground that may dispose of the case is preferred, and a single judge may decide it; requiring a three-judge court only to send it back would be inefficient. Mine Workers v. Gibbs concerns discretion over pendent state claims and did not displace this practice, and there is special reason to hear a claim that is in reality a Supremacy Clause claim, since \"federal courts are particularly appropriate bodies for the application of pre-emption principles.\" The Court left open whether other grounds also supported jurisdiction.","separate":"Justice Powell, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justice Rehnquist, dissented, joining Justice Rehnquist's dissent and adding that the majority misread Gibbs and Siler, and that the federal claim must have more than a glimmer of merit before a district court decides a pendent claim. Justice Rehnquist, joined by the Chief Justice and Justice Powell, dissented. He argued that the equal protection claim was insubstantial under Dandridge and was pleaded mainly to obtain jurisdiction over the stronger Supremacy Clause claim, which Congress had left to state courts through the amount-in-controversy requirement of section 1331. He added that, even if jurisdiction existed, the District Court abused its discretion under Gibbs by exercising pendent jurisdiction, and should have referred the constitutional claim to a three-judge court.","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals was reversed and the case remanded for further proceedings consistent with the opinion. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Douglas, Brennan, Stewart, Marshall and Blackmun. Justices Powell, Rehnquist and Chief Justice Burger dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep415/usrep415528/usrep415528.pdf"},"1970/203":{"facts":"McGautha was convicted in California of first-degree murder (and two armed robberies) after he and a codefendant held up two stores on the same day and a store owner was fatally shot; the evidence was inconclusive on who fired the shot. Crampton was convicted in Ohio of first-degree murder of his wife, whom he had threatened, after stealing guns and returning to her home; she was found shot in the face. In both states the jury had absolute discretion to choose death or life imprisonment, and the jurors received no standards for the choice. California tried guilt and punishment in two stages before the same jury, and McGautha and his codefendant testified at the penalty stage; the jury sentenced McGautha to death and his codefendant to life. Ohio tried guilt and punishment together in one proceeding and one verdict; Crampton did not testify, and the jury returned a guilty verdict with no recommendation of mercy, which carried a death sentence.","procedure":"The California Supreme Court unanimously affirmed McGautha's conviction and rejected his challenge to standardless jury sentencing, relying on an earlier narrowly divided decision. The Ohio Supreme Court affirmed Crampton's conviction and sentence. The Supreme Court granted certiorari in McGautha limited to the standards question, and in Crampton on that question plus the constitutionality of a single guilt-and-punishment proceeding.","question":"Does the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment forbid a State from leaving the choice between death and life imprisonment to the jury's absolute discretion, without governing standards? And is a single trial and verdict on both guilt and punishment in a capital case unconstitutional because it forces a defendant to choose between his privilege against self-incrimination on guilt and his opportunity to be heard on punishment?","holding":"No to both. Giving a capital jury \"untrammeled discretion of the jury\" over life or death does not violate the Constitution, and the Constitution does not require bifurcated capital trials. A State may decide that issues of guilt and punishment are better resolved in a single proceeding.","reasoning":"Justice Harlan began by stressing that the Court's role was to ask what the Constitution prohibits, not to impose a preferred system on the States. Tracing the history of capital punishment, he found a long record of failed attempts to define in advance which homicides deserve death, which led legislatures to hand the decision to juries. Every American jurisdiction that has used jury sentencing has done so without standards, and no court had held that unconstitutional. The Court reasoned that the proposed Model Penal Code criteria give only minimal control and show how intractable it is to write standards, so it found it impossible to say that standardless discretion offends the Constitution. On bifurcation, the Court distinguished Simmons v. United States and said the Constitution does not always forbid making a defendant choose, citing \"the making of difficult judgments\". It reasoned that the self-incrimination privilege is not offended when a defendant testifies on punishment at the risk of hurting his case on guilt, comparing cross-examination, impeachment, and other routine choices. As for the right to be heard on sentence, Ohio allowed counsel to argue for mercy, admitted broad background evidence, and kept allocution, so Crampton was not denied any constitutionally required opportunity.","separate":"Justice Black concurred in the judgments and substantially all of the opinion, but said the Court's task is not to decide whether trials were fairly conducted; he found no due process violation and no Eighth Amendment bar to capital punishment, since it was common when the Amendment was adopted. Justice Douglas, joined by Justices Brennan and Marshall, dissented in No. 204: Ohio's unitary trial violates procedural due process because a defendant can be heard on insanity or mercy only by giving up his privilege against self-incrimination, and Ohio bars evidence directed solely at mercy. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Douglas and Marshall, dissented in both cases: due process requires States to make explicit their basic policy choices and to use procedures that control arbitrary action and permit meaningful review, and he called the California and Ohio schemes \"stark legislative abdication\" that nothing in the nature of capital sentencing makes impossible to cure.","disposition":"Affirmed in both No. 203 (McGautha v. California) and No. 204 (Crampton v. Ohio). Justice Harlan delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White, and Blackmun; Justice Black filed a separate opinion concurring; Justices Douglas, Brennan, and Marshall dissented (Douglas only in No. 204).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep402/usrep402183/usrep402183.pdf"},"1972/71-6278":{"facts":"Almeida-Sanchez, a Mexican citizen with a valid U.S. work permit, was stopped by a Border Patrol roving patrol on a California state highway about 25 air miles north of the Mexican border, and his car was thoroughly searched. The agents had no warrant, no probable cause, no consent, and not even reasonable suspicion. The search turned up a large quantity of marihuana, and he was convicted of receiving, concealing, and facilitating the transportation of illegally imported marihuana. The Government relied on section 287(a)(3) of the Immigration and Nationality Act, which allows warrantless searches of vehicles within a reasonable distance of the border, and a regulation defining that as 100 air miles.","procedure":"The Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit recognized the search was not a border search but upheld it on the basis of the statute and regulation. The Supreme Court granted certiorari to consider the search's constitutionality.","question":"Does the Fourth Amendment permit the Border Patrol to search a vehicle, without a warrant, probable cause, or consent, on a road well inside the country some distance from the border, under a statute authorizing warrantless searches within a reasonable distance of the border?","holding":"No. A warrantless vehicle search by a roving patrol, made without probable cause or consent at a place that is neither the border nor its functional equivalent, violates the Fourth Amendment, and the statute cannot authorize it.","reasoning":"The Court first found no support in the automobile search cases, which require probable cause even without a warrant. It then rejected the analogy to administrative inspections: Camara required a warrant or consent to limit the discretion of the official in the field, and Colonnade and Biswell involved businesses in closely regulated, licensed industries whose owners accept inspection as a condition of the trade. The petitioner was not in any such business, and there was no reason to think he or his car had crossed the border. That left only the statute, but the Court stated that \"no Act of Congress can authorize a violation of the Constitution.\" Border searches may occur at the border or its functional equivalents, such as an established station where roads from the border meet or an arriving nonstop international flight, but this roving patrol search was of a wholly different sort. The seriousness of illegal immigration did not justify dispensing with constitutional safeguards, and the Court noted Carroll's statement that people lawfully within the country have a right to free passage absent probable cause.","separate":"Justice Powell concurred, joining the Court's opinion but adding that he would accept a functional equivalent of probable cause for roving border-area searches based on area-wide factors such as the frequency of alien transport, proximity to the border, and the road's character, while still requiring an area warrant from a judicial officer; since none was obtained here, he agreed the judgment must be reversed. Justice White, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Blackmun and Rehnquist, dissented. He would have held that the Fourth Amendment's standard is reasonableness, that Congress's judgment in the statute and the consistent views of the courts of appeals deserve weight, that the border is better treated as a zone than a line, and that the stop and limited search for aliens here was reasonable, making the discovery of marihuana lawful.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Douglas, Brennan, Marshall, and Powell (5-4), with Justice Powell also concurring.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep413/usrep413266/usrep413266.pdf"},"1976/75-1861":{"facts":"Gordon Patterson, estranged from his wife Roberta, borrowed a rifle, went to his father-in-law's home, and saw his wife in a state of semiundress with John Northrup, a neighbor to whom she had once been engaged. He entered and shot Northrup twice in the head, killing him. Patterson confessed before trial. He was charged with second-degree murder, which in New York requires intent to cause death and causing death, and at trial he raised the affirmative defense of extreme emotional disturbance, which reduces murder to manslaughter.","procedure":"The jury was instructed that the prosecution must prove each element beyond a reasonable doubt and that Patterson had to prove extreme emotional disturbance by a preponderance of the evidence. It convicted him of murder, and the Appellate Division affirmed. The New York Court of Appeals rejected his argument, based on Mullaney v. Wilbur, that the statute unconstitutionally shifted the burden of proof, and the Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction on appeal.","question":"Does the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment bar New York from requiring a murder defendant to prove by a preponderance of the evidence the affirmative defense of extreme emotional disturbance that reduces the crime to manslaughter?","holding":"No. Due process requires the prosecution to prove beyond a reasonable doubt every element of the offense as the State defines it, but a State need not disprove an affirmative defense that does not negate any element of the crime, and it may place the burden of proving such a defense on the defendant.","reasoning":"The Court noted that the State proved beyond a reasonable doubt each fact it made necessary for murder: the death, the intent to kill, and causation. Nothing further was presumed against Patterson, and extreme emotional disturbance did not negate any of those facts but was a separate issue on which the defendant carries the burden. That follows Leland v. Oregon and Rivera v. Delaware, which allowed placing the burden of proving insanity on the defendant, and common law placed the burden of proving heat of passion and similar defenses on the defendant. The Court said that \"Proof of the nonexistence of all affirmative defenses has never been constitutionally required\" and that a State that chooses to recognize a mitigating factor may assure itself the fact is established with reasonable certainty. It acknowledged that legislatures cannot simply relabel elements as affirmative defenses to evade Winship, and that there are constitutional limits. It distinguished Mullaney, where Maine's law presumed malice, an ingredient of murder as defined there, and required the defendant to rebut it; Mullaney forbids shifting the burden on an ingredient of the offense by presuming it, but did not reach this situation.","separate":"Justice Powell, joined by Justices Brennan and Marshall, dissented. He argued that the decision drains Winship and Mullaney of vitality by drawing a formalistic line based on how a statute is worded, since extreme emotional disturbance is the modern equivalent of the heat of passion at issue in Mullaney and the effect on the defendant is the same. He would hold that the prosecution must bear the burden of persuasion on a factor that makes a substantial difference in punishment and stigma and has historically held that importance, and he noted the State may still shift the burden of production.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Blackmun, and Stevens; Justices Powell, Brennan, and Marshall dissented. Justice Rehnquist took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep432/usrep432197/usrep432197.pdf"},"1969/830":{"facts":"Shortly after a Gulf service station in North Braddock, Pennsylvania was robbed by two armed men, police stopped a light blue station wagon matching the description given by teenage witnesses and the attendant. It carried four men, one in a green sweater and with a trench coat in the car. Petitioner was one of them. The occupants were arrested and the car was driven to the police station, where a thorough warrantless search found two revolvers, a glove of coins, and cards bearing the name of an attendant at another station robbed a week earlier. The next day a warrant-authorized search of petitioner's home turned up ammunition. Both attendants identified petitioner, and he was convicted of both robberies at a second trial; a Legal Aid attorney who first met him just before that trial represented him.","procedure":"Petitioner did not take a direct appeal. He sought habeas corpus in the Pennsylvania courts, which denied relief, and then in federal district court, which denied the petition without a hearing; the Third Circuit affirmed, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did the warrantless search of the automobile at the police station, after the occupants' arrest, violate the Fourth Amendment so that the items found had to be excluded? Also, were the admission of the ammunition seized from petitioner's home and the late substitution of counsel grounds for reversal?","holding":"The warrantless station-house search of the car was valid. Where police have probable cause to search an automobile, they may either search it immediately without a warrant or seize and hold it until a warrant is obtained; the Fourth Amendment treats the two courses alike because of the difference between cars and houses. Any error in admitting the home-seized ammunition was harmless, and the Court would not require a hearing or reversal for tardy appointment of counsel on this record.","reasoning":"The Court agreed the police had probable cause to stop the car and arrest its occupants, but held the later station-house search could not be justified as incident to arrest, because a search at another place after the accused is in custody is not incident to the arrest. It found another ground: the police had probable cause to believe the robbers, with guns and the fruits of the crime, were in this very station wagon, and under Carroll v. United States and later cases an automobile may be searched without a warrant on probable cause in circumstances that would not justify a warrantless search of a house or office, because the car is mobile and the chance to search is fleeting. Because the circumstances giving probable cause are usually unforeseeable, either the car must be searched at once or seized and held until a warrant issues, and the Court saw no constitutional difference between the two, since probable cause and mobility continued at the station house. The Court noted that \"there is a constitutional difference between houses and cars.\" On the ammunition, it accepted the lower courts' finding of harmless error. On counsel, it declined to adopt a per se rule against tardy appointment, noting that the Court of Appeals found no prejudice because the car search was valid and the bullet error harmless.","separate":"Justice Stewart concurred, adhering to his view that admitting evidence obtained in violation of the Fourth Amendment is not by itself a ground for collateral attack on a final conviction, but joining the Court because the Court had not adopted that view. Justice Harlan concurred in part and dissented in part. He would have required an evidentiary hearing on the ineffective-assistance claim, given counsel's last-minute entry and his handling of the suppression issue and cross-examination. He also dissented on the car search, arguing that the exception to the warrant requirement should be no broader than the exigency, so that police should only immobilize the car while seeking a warrant, which he thought the lesser intrusion; he agreed that admitting the bullets was harmless.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Blackmun took no part. The text does not state the vote count; Justice Stewart concurred and Justice Harlan concurred in part and dissented in part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep399/usrep399042/usrep399042.pdf"},"1975/74-728":{"facts":"Franks brought a class action under Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964 against Bowman Transportation Co. and its unions, alleging racially discriminatory employment practices. Lee intervened for himself and others, alleging discriminatory hiring and discharge policies limited to over-the-road (OTR) truck drivers. The District Court found a pattern of racial discrimination in hiring, transfer and discharge. It defined a class of black nonemployee applicants for OTR jobs before January 1, 1972 (class 3), whose applications were in evidence, and Lee had been hired later and then properly discharged for cause.","procedure":"The District Court enjoined the discriminatory practices and ordered Bowman to notify class members of their right to priority consideration, but denied the unnamed members backpay and retroactive seniority. The Court of Appeals vacated as to backpay but affirmed denial of any seniority relief to class 3, holding it barred as a matter of law by § 703(h) of Title VII. The Supreme Court granted certiorari on the seniority issue only.","question":"Is the case moot because the named class representative no longer has a personal stake? Does § 703(h) bar retroactive seniority relief to identifiable applicants refused employment because of race after Title VII took effect, and is such relief otherwise appropriate under § 706(g)?","holding":"The case is not moot, § 703(h) does not bar seniority relief for post-Act victims of discriminatory refusal to hire, and retroactive seniority dating from the job application is a form of relief generally appropriate under § 706(g). Courts should start from a presumption in favor of such relief and may deny it only for reasons that would not frustrate Title VII's purposes if applied generally.","reasoning":"A live controversy remained because, in a certified class action, an adversary relationship continued between Bowman and the unnamed class members, who were identifiable, still wanted seniority, and some were already working as OTR drivers. Section 703(h) appears to be a definitional provision aimed at attacks on seniority systems that perpetuate pre-Act discrimination, and its legislative history shows no intent to limit remedies once a post-Act discriminatory practice is proved. Section 706(g) gives courts broad equitable power to serve Title VII's objective \"to make persons whole for injuries suffered on account of unlawful employment discrimination\". Hiring without seniority falls short, because the victim would stay subordinate in layoffs, job bidding, vacations and pensions, and NLRA practice, the model for § 706(g), routinely pairs hiring relief with retroactive seniority. The District Court's reasons failed: lack of EEOC charges by class members was rejected in Albemarle Paper, concerns about vacancy and qualification arise only when individuals reapply and Bowman bears the burden then, and harm to other employees' expectations cannot alone justify denial. The Court noted that district courts keep discretion to modify relief for circumstances peculiar to a case.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred in part and dissented in part, agreeing generally with Justice Powell but stressing that competitive-type seniority relief at the expense of innocent employees can rarely be equitable, and that a monetary award such as front pay would be better. Justice Powell, joined by Justice Rehnquist, concurred in Part I and in the holding of Part II, but dissented from Parts III and IV: benefit-type seniority is like backpay, but competitive-type seniority falls on innocent incumbents rather than the employer, so § 706(g)'s grant of equitable discretion requires district courts to weigh the equities case by case, and he would remand for that weighing.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded to the District Court for further proceedings. Justice Brennan delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Stewart, White, Marshall and Blackmun, and in Part I by Justice Powell. Justice Stevens took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep424/usrep424747/usrep424747.pdf"},"1973/72-1598":{"facts":"A union petitioned the NLRB for an election among 25 buyers in the purchasing department of Bell Aerospace's Wheatfield, New York plant. Buyers had broad discretion to select vendors, negotiate terms and sign purchase orders up to $50,000, subject to approval above $5,000 and to team input on some projects. The company argued the buyers were \"managerial employees\" and so not covered by the National Labor Relations Act.","procedure":"The Board, relying on its North Arkansas decision, held that managerial employees are covered unless union membership would create a conflict of interest in labor relations, found no such conflict shown, directed an election, certified the union, and ordered the company to bargain after it refused. The Second Circuit denied enforcement, holding that Congress excluded all true managerial employees and that, since the Board had long held buyers managerial, it could change position only through rulemaking, and remanded. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the NLRA exclude all \"managerial employees\" from its protections, or only those whose union membership would create a conflict of interest in labor relations? And must the Board use rulemaking rather than adjudication to decide whether buyers are managerial employees?","holding":"Congress intended to exclude all employees properly classified as managerial from the Act, not just those with labor-relations conflicts of interest. The Board is not required to proceed by rulemaking rather than adjudication in deciding whether these buyers are managerial.","reasoning":"The Court relied on the Board's early decisions treating managerial employees as outside bargaining units and never certifying a unit of them, and on the Taft-Hartley legislative history. The 1947 reports show concern about unionizing management, and the conference committee treated labor-relations and confidential employees as impliedly excluded, which the Court read to include executives and other managerial employees. The Board consistently held managerial employees outside the Act for over two decades, Congress left that reading untouched in the 1959 amendments, and the courts of appeals uniformly approved it, so the Board could not adopt a narrower exclusion. On rulemaking, SEC v. Chenery II and NLRB v. Wyman-Gordon establish that an agency may announce new principles in adjudication and that the choice lies in its informed discretion. Adjudication suits this context because of the very large number of buyers and the wide variation in their duties, and an agency should not \"exalt form over necessity.\" The Court expressed no view on whether these buyers are in fact managerial.","separate":"Justice White, joined by Justices Brennan, Stewart and Marshall, concurred in Part III (no rulemaking required) but dissented from Part II. He argued that the Act covers \"any employee\" except those expressly excluded, that supervisors and professionals were addressed specifically while managerial employees were not, that before 1947 the Board excluded managerial employees only from rank-and-file units and had never held them outside the Act, and that congressional silence does not show approval of the Board's later Swift decision, so the Board's narrower exclusion was permissible.","disposition":"Affirmed in part, reversed in part, and remanded (to the Court of Appeals with directions to remand to the Board). Justice Powell delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Douglas, Blackmun and Rehnquist.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep416/usrep416267/usrep416267.pdf"},"1969/540":{"facts":"New York administered the federally supported Aid to Families With Dependent Children (AFDC) program. After Congress added a requirement in 42 U.S.C. 602(a)(23) that States adjust their standards of need for changes in living costs by July 1, 1969, New York enacted section 131-a of its Social Services Law. The new law replaced individualized grants and special-needs grants with maximum family allowances based on family size and the average age of the oldest child. The change cut benefits to many recipients, and cut New York City benefits by almost $40,000,000. Welfare recipients sued, first on an equal protection claim about unequal Nassau County payments, with a pendent claim that the new state law conflicted with the federal statute.","procedure":"A three-judge court found the equal protection claim moot after the State amended the law, dissolved itself, and remanded to the single District Judge. The District Judge decided the statutory claim for the recipients and issued an injunction. The Court of Appeals reversed, holding that the District Judge should not have reached the merits and that the federal statute did not affect New York. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Could the District Court hear the federal statutory claim as a pendent claim after the original constitutional claim became moot, and if so, is New York's new welfare program consistent with section 402(a)(23) of the Social Security Act?","holding":"The District Court had jurisdiction and properly exercised its discretion to reach the merits; neither exhaustion of administrative remedies nor primary jurisdiction required deferral to HEW. Section 402(a)(23) invalidates a state program that substantially reduces the content of the standard of need unless the State shows the dropped items no longer formed part of the reality of existence for most recipients, and New York's elimination of special grants failed that test.","reasoning":"On jurisdiction, the Court said mootness of the primary claim goes to the court's discretion, not its power, and that judicial energy already spent and the federal character of the statutory claim favored deciding it. It found the administrative doctrines inapplicable because recipients seek no review of an agency order and HEW had no procedure by which they could participate. On the merits, the Court read the statute as having two aims: to make States face the real magnitude of need and to push them to apportion payments more equitably. A State may reduce the percentage of need it pays, but it “may not obscure the actual standard of need.” It rejected both the District Court's broad reading and the Court of Appeals' view that the statute did not affect New York. The record, including the State's own regulations and its 1968 pilot project, showed that special grants covered essentials such as laundry and telephones, so their elimination significantly cut the content of the standard. Averaging of items remained permissible. Because compliance would be very costly and no single provision could be severed, the Court remanded so New York could revise its program, with an injunction against use of federal funds if it did not.","separate":"Justice Douglas concurred, adding that the federal statutory claim was within the pendent jurisdiction of the three-judge court, that the discretionary factors of economy, convenience and fairness favored exercising it, and that recipients cannot be required to exhaust remedies before HEW because HEW gives them no way to participate. Justice Black, joined by the Chief Justice, dissented. He argued that Congress gave HEW primary responsibility for deciding whether state plans conform, that HEW was already reviewing the New York law, and that federal courts should not rule on such conflicts before a final HEW decision.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded to the Court of Appeals for further proceedings consistent with the opinion, with directions that the District Court set a date for New York to revise its program. Justice Harlan delivered the opinion of the Court; the text shown does not state a vote count, but Justice Douglas concurred and Justice Black, joined by the Chief Justice, dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep397/usrep397397/usrep397397.pdf"},"1970/338":{"facts":"The University of Illinois Foundation owned the Isbell patent on a television antenna. In an earlier suit against another manufacturer, Winegard, a federal district court held the patent invalid as obvious, and the Eighth Circuit affirmed. The Foundation separately sued a customer of Blonder-Tongue Laboratories in the Northern District of Illinois, and Blonder-Tongue defended and counterclaimed. That court disagreed with the Winegard result and held the patent valid and infringed.","procedure":"The Seventh Circuit affirmed the finding that the Isbell patent was valid and infringed, creating a conflict with the Eighth Circuit. The Supreme Court granted certiorari and asked the parties to address whether Triplett v. Lowell should be adhered to and, if not, whether the Winegard invalidity ruling bound the Foundation.","question":"Should the Triplett rule, that a judgment of patent invalidity is not binding on the patentee in later suits against different defendants, be maintained, or may an accused infringer plead estoppel based on an earlier declaration of invalidity?","holding":"Triplett is overruled to the extent that it forecloses an estoppel plea by one charged with infringing a patent already declared invalid. The patentee may show that he lacked a full and fair chance to litigate validity in the first case, and trial courts decide on the basis of justice and equity.","reasoning":"The Court traced Triplett to the doctrine of mutuality of estoppel and described how many courts had abandoned that doctrine, especially since the California decision in Bernhard. It asked whether it is still tenable to give a litigant more than one full and fair opportunity to litigate the same issue. It rejected the argument that patent cases are so complex that repeated litigation is needed as a safeguard, noting that the patentee chose the first forum and could present all its evidence. It stressed the economic costs of Triplett: patent suits are expensive, patents are presumed valid, and accused infringers often settle or take licenses rather than relitigate, which hurts competitors and consumers. The burden on the courts was a lesser consideration. The patentee must be allowed to show it lacked “a fair opportunity procedurally, substantively and evidentially to pursue his claim the first time.” Because Triplett had discouraged Blonder-Tongue from pleading estoppel, the Court remanded so the pleadings could be amended and the record supplemented.","disposition":"Judgment of the Court of Appeals vacated and the case remanded to the District Court for further proceedings. Justice White delivered the opinion for a unanimous Court.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep402/usrep402313/usrep402313.pdf"},"1976/75-1153":{"facts":"Michigan law allowed a union and a local government employer to agree to an agency shop, under which every represented employee, whether or not a union member, must pay the union a service charge equal to dues. The Detroit Federation of Teachers and the Detroit Board of Education agreed to such a clause, and a teacher who did not pay could be discharged. Teachers who were unwilling to pay, who opposed public-sector collective bargaining, or who objected to union political and ideological activities sued in state court. They alleged that the union spent part of the fees on economic, political, professional, scientific and religious activities they did not approve, unrelated to collective bargaining.","procedure":"The trial court dismissed the complaints for failure to state a claim. The Michigan Court of Appeals upheld the agency-shop clause on its face under Railway Employes' Dept. v. Hanson, but held that the teachers could get no restitution because they had not told the union which causes and candidates they objected to. The teachers appealed to the Supreme Court.","question":"Does the First and Fourteenth Amendments' protection of freedom of association bar a public employer from requiring non-member employees to pay an agency-shop service charge to a union, either at all or to the extent the money funds union activities unrelated to collective bargaining?","holding":"A public-sector agency shop is constitutional insofar as the fees finance collective bargaining, contract administration and grievance adjustment. The Constitution does not allow a public employee to be compelled, as a condition of employment, to support union ideological or political causes unrelated to those duties.","reasoning":"The Court treated Hanson and Street, which upheld union shops under the Railway Labor Act, as controlling for bargaining-related expenses, because the same interests in exclusive representation, labor peace and preventing free riders apply in the public sector. It rejected the arguments that government employment calls for a different result and that public-sector bargaining is so political that any compelled support is ideological conformity, finding that public employees' First Amendment interests are no weightier and that they remain free to express their views and take part in politics. On the second question, it held that compelling contributions for political purposes infringes First Amendment freedom of belief and association just as prohibiting them would. The Constitution requires only that ideological expenditures not germane to collective bargaining be financed by those who do not object. The Court did not draw the line between the two categories on this bare pleading record. As to remedy, it held that the state court erred in requiring objectors to name specific expenditures, following Railway Clerks v. Allen, and that remedies such as a refund or reduction of fees proportional to ideological spending might be available. It suggested that the parties might first use the union's newly adopted internal refund procedure.","separate":"Justice Rehnquist concurred, joining the Court only because he believes the Elrod v. Burns dissent states the governing principles, and agreeing that public-sector bargaining positions touch political concerns. Justice Stevens concurred, saying that by joining he did not imply that the Street and Allen remedies would necessarily be adequate, and that the union may be required to adopt procedures that avoid even temporary use of objectors' funds for ideological activities. Justice Powell, joined by the Chief Justice and Justice Blackmun, concurred in the judgment. He agreed that the complaints state a cause of action but argued that Hanson and Street do not control because here the government itself is the employer, that any compelled support of a public-sector union implicates the First Amendment, and that the State should bear the burden of showing that each compelled contribution serves paramount governmental interests.","disposition":"Judgment of the Michigan Court of Appeals vacated and the case remanded for further proceedings not inconsistent with the opinion. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, White, Marshall, Rehnquist and Stevens.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep431/usrep431209/usrep431209.pdf"},"1976/75-6521":{"facts":"Donald Abney, Larry Starks and Alonzo Robinson were indicted with two others in a single count charging conspiracy and attempt to obstruct interstate commerce by extortion in violation of the Hobbs Act. The Government's case rested on a tavern owner who said the defendants had extorted weekly payments from him and who recorded the last payment. Petitioners argued that the single count was duplicitous. The trial court did not dismiss it or make the prosecutor choose, but required proof of all elements of both offenses and instructed the jury so. The jury convicted each petitioner.","procedure":"The Third Circuit reversed the convictions because the tape recording had not been properly authenticated, agreed that the indictment was duplicitous, and ordered the Government to elect between conspiracy and attempt on retrial. The Government elected conspiracy, and petitioners moved to dismiss on double jeopardy grounds and for failure to charge an offense. The District Court denied the motion, and the Court of Appeals affirmed on an immediate appeal without addressing its jurisdiction. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Is a pretrial order denying a motion to dismiss an indictment on double jeopardy grounds a “final decision” under 28 U.S.C. 1291 that may be appealed immediately, and if so, does double jeopardy bar this retrial and may the court of appeals also review petitioners' other challenge to the indictment?","holding":"A pretrial order rejecting a double jeopardy claim is a final decision under section 1291 and may be appealed immediately, under the collateral order exception. Other claims in the same motion, such as the sufficiency of the indictment, are not immediately appealable. On the merits, double jeopardy did not bar retrial on the conspiracy charge.","reasoning":"The Court began with the principle that appeals are creatures of statute and that Congress has a firm policy against piecemeal appeals. It then applied the Cohen collateral order test. The denial of a double jeopardy claim is a complete and final rejection in the trial court, and the claim is collateral to and separable from guilt or innocence. The Court also reasoned that the Double Jeopardy Clause protects against being tried twice, not only against being punished twice, so the right would be significantly undermined if review waited until after a second trial. Petitioners' attack on the sufficiency of the indictment is not collateral, goes to the heart of the trial, and can be reviewed after final judgment, so the Court of Appeals lacked jurisdiction over it at this stage. On the merits, the trial court had instructed the jury that it must find all elements of both conspiracy and attempt, and the Court would not assume the jury disregarded that instruction. The jury therefore did not acquit petitioners of conspiracy, and retrial on that charge is not barred.","separate":"Justice White concurred in the judgment without a written opinion in the text shown.","disposition":"Affirmed in part, reversed in part, and remanded. Chief Justice Burger delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, Stewart, Marshall, Blackmun, Powell, Rehnquist and Stevens; Justice White concurred in the judgment.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep431/usrep431651/usrep431651.pdf"},"1969/768":{"facts":"Boys Markets and Retail Clerks Union Local 770 were parties to a collective-bargaining agreement that required all disputes over its interpretation or application to go through grievance and arbitration procedures and barred any work stoppage, lockout, picketing or boycott during the contract. A dispute arose when the company's frozen foods supervisor and non-unit crew members began rearranging merchandise in a supermarket's frozen food cases, and a union representative demanded that union personnel restock the cases. When the company refused, the union struck and picketed. The company demanded an end to the stoppage and tried to invoke the contract's arbitration procedures.","procedure":"The company sued in California Superior Court and obtained a temporary restraining order. The union removed the case to federal district court, which ordered arbitration and enjoined the strike and picketing. The Ninth Circuit reversed, considering itself bound by Sinclair Refining Co. v. Atkinson, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the Norris-LaGuardia Act's ban on federal injunctions in labor disputes bar a federal court from enjoining a strike that breaches a no-strike clause when the underlying dispute is subject to binding arbitration under a collective-bargaining agreement, and should Sinclair v. Atkinson be overruled?","holding":"The Court overruled Sinclair and held that the Norris-LaGuardia Act does not bar an injunction against a strike over an arbitrable grievance in these circumstances. The holding is narrow and applies where the contract has a mandatory grievance or arbitration procedure; the district court must first order arbitration and must find that ordinary principles of equity support an injunction.","reasoning":"Justice Brennan said stare decisis is a policy rather than a mechanical formula and that congressional silence after Sinclair is not a reason to refuse reconsideration. He reasoned that Avco, which allows removal of Section 301(a) suits from state to federal court, combined with Sinclair to strip state courts of injunction power in such suits, contrary to Congress's purpose to supplement rather than displace state jurisdiction. Extending Sinclair to the States would instead remove the principal enforcement tool for no-strike pledges, which are the quid pro quo for an employer's agreement to arbitrate, and damages are no substitute for an immediate halt to a strike. Statutes must be read together, and the Norris-LaGuardia Act was aimed at abuses of injunctions against organizing, not at enforcing a union's own contractual promise to arbitrate rather than strike, as in Chicago River. The Court adopted the Sinclair dissent's guidelines: arbitration must be ordered as a condition of the injunction, and the court must weigh irreparable injury and the balance of hardships. It stated that \"Our holding in the present case is a narrow one.\"","separate":"Justice Stewart concurred, explaining that he had joined Sinclair in 1962 but later concluded it should be reconsidered, and that he now joins the Court's opinion. Justice Black dissented, arguing that Sinclair was correctly decided, that Congress had been invited to change the law and declined, and that changing a settled statutory interpretation because the Court's membership changed is a legislative act. Justice White dissented for the reasons stated in the majority opinion in Sinclair. Justice Marshall took no part.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded with directions to enter a judgment affirming the District Court's order. Justice Brennan delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Marshall took no part, Justice Stewart concurred, and Justices Black and White dissented. The text does not state a vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep398/usrep398235/usrep398235.pdf"},"1971/70-69":{"facts":"Orito was indicted for knowingly transporting obscene film material by common carrier in interstate commerce, from San Francisco to Milwaukee by two airlines, in violation of 18 U.S.C. section 1462. The materials included about 83 reels of film, with several copies of some. He moved to dismiss, arguing the statute violated his First and Ninth Amendment rights.","procedure":"The District Court granted the motion and held the statute unconstitutionally overbroad because it did not distinguish between public and nonpublic transportation of obscene material, relying on Griswold, Redrup and Stanley v. Georgia. The United States appealed directly to the Supreme Court, and the case was argued and then reargued.","question":"Does the First Amendment, through the privacy right recognized in Stanley v. Georgia, bar Congress from prohibiting the interstate transportation of obscene material by common carrier, even when the transport is private or the material is for the transporter's own use?","holding":"No. Congress may regulate the interstate transportation of obscene material, which the First Amendment does not protect, and the privacy zone of Stanley v. Georgia does not extend beyond the home. The District Court's dismissal on privacy grounds was error, and the case was remanded to reconsider the sufficiency of the indictment in light of Miller v. California and related decisions.","reasoning":"Chief Justice Burger rejected the argument that Stanley's right to possess obscene material at home creates a correlative right to receive, transport or distribute it, noting that Thirty-seven Photographs and Reidel negate any zone of privacy that follows such material outside the home. He wrote that \"The Constitution extends special safeguards to the privacy of the home,\" but that viewing obscene films in a commercial theater or shipping them on common carriers has no claim to such consideration. Three points carried the result: \"obscene material is not protected under the First Amendment,\" the government has a legitimate interest in keeping such material out of the stream of commerce, and no constitutionally protected privacy is involved. A transporter's private use or private carriage is not controlling, because Congress may rely on the legislatively determined risk of eventual exposure to juveniles or the public. Because the case came up on a summary dismissal, the obscenity of the films was never determined, and the Court noted that for the first time since Roth it had majority-accepted standards for obscenity in Miller and 12 200-ft. Reels.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented, reasoning that under Stanley a person who reads or carries an obscene book on a journey for personal enjoyment is protected, so section 1462 is overbroad, and he would affirm unless Stanley is overruled. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Stewart and Marshall, dissented, relying on his dissent in Paris Adult Theatre I v. Slaton and Miller to conclude that the statute is overbroad and unconstitutional on its face, and he would affirm.","disposition":"Vacated and remanded. Chief Justice Burger delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices White, Blackmun, Powell and Rehnquist; Justices Douglas, Brennan, Stewart and Marshall dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep413/usrep413139/usrep413139.pdf"},"1974/74-165":{"facts":"Eight major railroads in the northeast and midwest entered reorganization under section 77 of the Bankruptcy Act, creating a rail transportation crisis. Congress responded with the Regional Rail Reorganization Act of 1973, which created the United States Railway Association (USRA) to draft a Final System Plan and required designated rail properties to be conveyed to a new private corporation, Conrail, in exchange for Conrail securities, up to $500 million of federally guaranteed USRA obligations, and other benefits. Section 304(f) restricted railroads from discontinuing service until the plan took effect. Creditors and the sole stockholder of Penn Central, along with trustees, challenged the Act, arguing it took property without just compensation through interim losses (the \"erosion taking\") and through the final conveyance (the \"conveyance taking\"), and that it violated the Bankruptcy Clause's uniformity requirement.","procedure":"A three-judge District Court for the Eastern District of Pennsylvania held the conveyance taking claim premature, but held the erosion claim ripe, found that the Rail Act precluded any Tucker Act remedy, and declared section 304(f) and section 303 invalid to the extent they required continued loss operations without compensation. It enjoined USRA from certifying the plan and held part of section 207(b) nonuniform under the Bankruptcy Clause. The United States, USRA and the Penn Central Trustees appealed directly, and the New Haven Trustee cross-appealed.","question":"Is a Tucker Act suit in the Court of Claims available, and adequate, to compensate any unconstitutional taking effected by the Rail Act through interim erosion or the final conveyance, which of the conveyance issues are ripe, and does the Act, which applies to railroads in one defined region, satisfy the uniformity requirement of the Bankruptcy Clause?","holding":"The Court reversed. The Rail Act does not withdraw the Tucker Act remedy, which is available to cover any compensation shortfall from either the erosion or the conveyance, and that remedy makes the Act valid as a reorganization statute that satisfies due process. The basic conveyance issues are ripe, though valuation questions are not, and the Act satisfies the Bankruptcy Clause's uniformity requirement.","reasoning":"Justice Brennan held the erosion issue ripe because Penn Central was not reorganizable on an income basis and compelled loss operations could erode creditors' interests with no assurance of compensation. He found that the District Court asked the wrong question, since the issue is whether Congress withdrew the Tucker Act's jurisdiction over claims founded on the Constitution, and the Act's text, funding provisions, section 601 and legislative history show no such withdrawal. Disfavored implied repeals and the canon of construing statutes to avoid constitutional doubt, with Lynch v. United States, point to the same result. On the conveyance, he held the basic issues ripe because the conveyance is virtually certain and irreversible, with no better time to decide, but held valuation methods premature. He rejected the claim that the Act is an eminent domain statute, treating it as an exercise of the bankruptcy power under which compensation in securities backed by a Tucker Act remedy is adequate. On uniformity, he held that Congress may address geographically isolated problems and that the Act applies to all railroads in reorganization, so that \"The uniformity clause requires that the Rail Act apply equally to all creditors and all debtors.\"","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented, concluding that the Act effects a taking without assured just compensation and amounts to a fraudulent conveyance, that the Rail Act's expedited structure and limits on federal funds show Congress meant to displace a Tucker Act remedy, that the Court's saving construction rewrites the statute, and that the Act lacks the geographic uniformity the Bankruptcy Clause requires. Justice Stewart dissented from the opinion and judgment substantially for the reasons in Part II of Douglas's dissent.","disposition":"Reversed (judgment of the three-judge District Court, 383 F. Supp. 510). Justice Brennan delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices White, Marshall, Blackmun, Powell and Rehnquist; Justices Douglas and Stewart dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep419/usrep419102/usrep419102.pdf"},"1969/85":{"facts":"Petitioners sell data processing services to businesses generally. They sued to challenge a ruling of the Comptroller of the Currency that national banks may, as an incident to their banking services, make data processing services available to other banks and bank customers. They alleged that competition from national banks would cost them profits and that respondent American National Bank & Trust Company was performing or preparing to perform such services for two customers with whom petitioner Data Systems, Inc., had agreed or negotiated.","procedure":"The District Court dismissed the complaint because petitioners lacked standing to sue. The Court of Appeals affirmed, applying a test that required a legal interest from a public charter, contract, or statute, or a public interest recognized by Congress. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Do competitors of national banks have standing under Article III and the Administrative Procedure Act to challenge the Comptroller's ruling, and has Congress precluded judicial review of that ruling?","holding":"Yes to both parts. The petitioners have standing: they allege injury in fact, and their interest is arguably within the zone of interests protected or regulated by the relevant statutes. Congress did not preclude judicial review of the Comptroller's ruling.","reasoning":"The Court separated the Article III inquiry from the merits. The first question is whether the challenged action caused the plaintiff injury in fact, and petitioners' alleged economic harm met that test. The Court of Appeals's \"legal interest\" test went to the merits and was not a proper standing test. The further question is whether the interest is \"arguably within the zone of interests to be protected or regulated by the statute,\" which fits the Administrative Procedure Act's grant of review to persons aggrieved by agency action. Section 4 of the Bank Service Corporation Act, which limits bank service corporations to bank services for banks, arguably brings a competitor within that zone. On preclusion, the Court read the APA as broadly remedial, saying there is \"no presumption against judicial review and in favor of administrative absolutism\" unless a contrary purpose is fairly discernible, and it found none in the Bank Service Corporation Act or the National Bank Act. Whether petitioners have a legal interest and whether the Comptroller violated the statutes are merits questions left for the lower courts.","separate":"The text notes that Justice Brennan filed a separate opinion (post, p. 167), but that opinion is not included in the provided text, so its content cannot be described.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for a hearing on the merits. Justice Douglas delivered the opinion of the Court; the opinion text does not state a vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep397/usrep397150/usrep397150.pdf"},"1970/133":{"facts":"Milton Luros returned from Europe on October 24, 1969, with 37 photographs in his luggage. Customs agents seized them as obscene under 19 U.S.C. § 1305(a), which bars importing obscene material and directs seizure and a forfeiture suit by the district attorney. The U.S. Attorney filed a forfeiture action on November 6. Luros denied obscenity and counterclaimed that the statute was unconstitutional, and the parties stipulated that the photographs were meant for a hard cover edition of The Kama Sutra of Vatsyayana.","procedure":"A three-judge District Court declared § 1305(a) unconstitutional on two grounds: it lacked the time limits required by Freedman v. Maryland, and under Stanley v. Georgia it was overbroad because it reached importation for private use. The Government appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"Does § 1305(a) satisfy the procedural requirements of Freedman v. Maryland, and does Stanley v. Georgia bar its application to obscene material that a traveler brings in from abroad?","holding":"The statute can be construed to require suit within 14 days of seizure and a final district court decision within 60 days of filing (excluding claimant-caused delay), which avoids the Freedman problem. Congress may bar importation of obscene material, including material intended for private use, and Stanley does not protect that importation.","reasoning":"Justice White noted that Freedman, Teitel and Blount struck down censorship schemes lacking prompt judicial review, but that the Court should first look for a fairly possible construction that avoids a constitutional question. Unlike the state laws in Freedman and Teitel, a federal statute can be construed authoritatively by this Court, and unlike the Blount statute it needed no wholesale rewriting. The Senate debates showed concern that courts decide promptly, so reading in time limits fits congressional purpose. Reviewing lower-court cases, the Court chose 14 and 60 days as workable, and the Government's filing 13 days after seizure met them. On Stanley, a port of entry is not a home, and since \"obscenity is not within the scope of First Amendment protection,\" Congress may declare it contraband and exclude it, as the Court also held in United States v. Reidel.","separate":"Justice Harlan concurred in the judgment and in Part I, and concurred only in the judgment on Part II. He would have held that Luros, who stipulated to commercial importation, lacked standing to raise the overbreadth claim for private importers. Justice Stewart concurred in the judgment and in Part I, agreed that border seizure of obscenity for commercial dissemination is permissible, but would not decide that the Government may seize material meant for the importer's purely private use. Justice Black, joined by Justice Douglas, dissented: he would deny Congress any power to censor, argued that the plurality's reasoning cannot be squared with Stanley, and said the Court improperly rewrote the statute instead of affirming. Justice Marshall's dissent appears in the companion case Reidel and is not included in this text.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice White announced the judgment and wrote the opinion; the Chief Justice and Justices Brennan and Blackmun joined all of it, and Justices Harlan and Stewart joined Part I only and concurred in the judgment.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep402/usrep402363/usrep402363.pdf"},"1975/75-251":{"facts":"Present and retired male employees of Connecticut sued state officials on behalf of a class, alleging that the state retirement plan discriminated on the basis of sex in violation of Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964. Congress had amended Title VII in 1972 to cover states as employers. The employees sought retroactive retirement benefits and attorneys' fees.","procedure":"The District Court found a Title VII violation and granted prospective injunctive relief, but denied retroactive benefits and fees as barred by the Eleventh Amendment under Edelman v. Jordan. The Court of Appeals affirmed on benefits but reversed on attorneys' fees, treating them as only an ancillary effect on the state treasury. The Supreme Court granted certiorari on both petitions.","question":"May Congress, acting under § 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment, authorize federal courts to award money damages against a state government despite the Eleventh Amendment?","holding":"Yes. The Eleventh Amendment and the state sovereignty it embodies are limited by § 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment, so Congress may provide for suits against states that would be impermissible in other contexts. The backpay award is therefore not barred, and neither is the fee award authorized by Title VII.","reasoning":"The Court began where Edelman ended: here the \"threshold fact of congressional authorization\" to sue the state as employer is present, since the parties agreed Congress acted under § 5. The Fourteenth Amendment's substantive provisions are directed at the States, and Ex parte Virginia and later cases recognized that Congress's enforcement power can intrude on spheres once reserved to the States. The Court concluded that the Eleventh Amendment and state sovereignty are \"necessarily limited by the enforcement provisions of § 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment.\" Because Title VII expressly authorizes fees, the same holding means Congress's exercise of power there is also not barred, so the Court did not decide the Edelman ancillary-effect question.","separate":"Justice Brennan concurred in the judgment, reasoning that the Eleventh Amendment does not literally apply to a suit by a state's own citizens and that the States surrendered sovereign immunity in the plan of the Convention as to Congress's enumerated powers. Justice Stevens concurred in the judgment, relying on the commerce power and Ex parte Young and doubting that § 5 adequately answers the defense; he thought Edelman's holding did not cover this award and would treat fees like other litigation costs.","disposition":"No. 75-251 reversed; No. 75-283 affirmed. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices Stewart, White, Marshall, Blackmun and Powell.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep427/usrep427445/usrep427445.pdf"},"1976/75-1053":{"facts":"Jones, a Riverside County weights and measures director, ordered Rath Packing Co.'s bacon and flour from three millers (General Mills, Pillsbury, Seaboard) off sale. He found, by the statistical sampling method of California's Article 5, that lot average net weights fell below the stated weights, in violation of Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code § 12211. California's method allows for manufacturing variation but not for moisture loss during good distribution practice. Federal law permits reasonable variations from stated weight, including moisture loss.","procedure":"Rath and the millers sued in federal district court for declarations that the California provisions were pre-empted and for injunctions. The District Court granted relief and the Court of Appeals affirmed in relevant part; the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Do the Federal Meat Inspection Act, the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act and the Fair Packaging and Labeling Act pre-empt California's net-weight enforcement scheme as applied to bacon and flour?","holding":"As to bacon, California's law is expressly pre-empted by the FMIA's ban on labeling requirements \"different than\" federal ones. As to flour, the FPLA's express clause does not pre-empt the state law, but enforcement would obstruct the FPLA's purposes and so it must yield under the Supremacy Clause.","reasoning":"The Court started from the presumption that historic state police powers are not displaced absent a clear congressional purpose, and noted that conflict pre-emption asks whether state law obstructs congressional objectives. For bacon, FMIA § 408 bars labeling requirements different than the federal ones, and California's refusal to allow moisture-loss variation differs from the federal rule; Jones's argument that accuracy requirements are not labeling requirements was rejected. For flour, the Court concluded that the FDCA and FPLA together also permit reasonable variations, and that the FPLA's \"less stringent\" and \"different information\" clauses did not reach the California law. It then held that a major FPLA purpose is to \"facilitate value comparisons among similar products,\" and that California's standard would force national millers to overpack and make identically labeled packages contain different amounts of flour solids, which would mislead consumers.","separate":"Justice Rehnquist, joined by Justice Stewart, concurred in part and dissented in part. He agreed with express pre-emption as to bacon and the absence of express pre-emption as to flour, but dissented from implied pre-emption of the flour claims, arguing that compliance with both laws is possible and that the majority relied on unproved factual speculation about milling and distribution.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Marshall wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices Brennan, White, Blackmun, Powell and Stevens.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep430/usrep430519/usrep430519.pdf"},"1977/76-419":{"facts":"Vermont Yankee sought an operating license for a nuclear plant, and Consumers Power a construction permit for two reactors. In the Vermont Yankee matter the Atomic Energy Commission excluded the environmental effects of fuel reprocessing and waste disposal from the licensing hearing, then held rulemaking proceedings without discovery or cross-examination and adopted a fuel cycle rule. In the Consumers Power matter, an intervenor group, Saginaw, raised energy conservation contentions but largely declined to participate, and the Commission refused to reopen the proceeding; intervenors also challenged the Advisory Committee on Reactor Safeguards report.","procedure":"The D.C. Circuit overturned the fuel cycle rule as procedurally inadequate and remanded the Vermont Yankee license. In the Consumers Power case it held the environmental impact statement defective for not examining energy conservation, held the ACRS report inadequate, and remanded. The Supreme Court granted certiorari in both cases.","question":"May a reviewing court require an agency to use rulemaking procedures beyond those in the Administrative Procedure Act, and did the Court of Appeals properly overturn the Commission's handling of energy conservation and the ACRS report?","holding":"Section 553 of the APA generally sets the maximum procedural requirements courts may impose on agency rulemaking, so the Court of Appeals could not overturn the rule for the procedures used. The court also erred in rejecting the Commission's energy conservation threshold test and in requiring the ACRS report to be returned for elaboration.","reasoning":"The Court said that APA § 553 \"established the maximum procedural requirements\" Congress was willing to have courts impose, that circumstances justifying more are \"extremely rare,\" and that agencies have long been left to design their own procedures. It found the Court of Appeals had in effect faulted the procedures, and that neither NEPA, the nature of the issues nor past practice justified more. Judicial insistence on the perceived best procedures would make review unpredictable and push agencies toward full adjudicatory hearings, and the adequacy of a rulemaking record turns on following the statutes, not on particular procedural devices. On energy conservation, the concept of alternatives is evolving and bounded by feasibility, and intervenors must make their contentions meaningful. The ACRS report remand was an unjustified intrusion. The Court remanded the fuel cycle rule for ordinary arbitrary-and-capricious review.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, in which all participating Members joined; Justices Blackmun and Powell took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep435/usrep435519/usrep435519.pdf"},"1978/77-752":{"facts":"Unions won Board-supervised elections as bargaining representatives of lay teachers at two groups of Catholic high schools: the Quigley schools run by the Catholic Bishop of Chicago and five schools of the Diocese of Fort Wayne-South Bend. The schools combine religious instruction with a secular curriculum. They refused to recognize or bargain with the unions.","procedure":"The NLRB asserted jurisdiction because the schools were not \"completely religious\" and ordered them to bargain. The Seventh Circuit denied enforcement, holding the Board's standard unworkable and that the Religion Clauses barred jurisdiction. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the National Labor Relations Act give the NLRB jurisdiction over lay teachers in church-operated schools that teach both religious and secular subjects, and if so, does exercising it violate the Religion Clauses?","holding":"The Act should not be construed to cover lay teachers in church-operated schools. Because jurisdiction would raise serious First Amendment questions and Congress expressed no affirmative intention to include them, the Board lacked authority to issue the orders.","reasoning":"Chief Justice Burger first addressed statutory coverage, applying the rule that an Act of Congress should not be read to violate the Constitution if another construction is available. Where a reading would raise serious constitutional questions, the Court requires \"the affirmative intention of the Congress clearly expressed.\" He found that exercising jurisdiction would present \"a significant risk that the First Amendment will be infringed,\" because the Board's inquiry into good faith and religious mission, and the scope of mandatory bargaining over terms and conditions of employment, would entangle it with church schools, as in Lemon, Meek and Wolman. The text and history of the 1935, 1947 and 1974 enactments show no such intention, and Associated Press v. NLRB was distinguished because no First Amendment problem appeared there. The Court therefore declined to construe the Act to reach these teachers and did not decide the constitutional claims.","separate":"Justice Brennan, joined by Justices White, Marshall and Blackmun, dissented. He argued that the Court's \"affirmative intention\" rule departs from the settled canon requiring only a fairly possible avoiding construction; that the Act covers all employers not expressly excepted; and that Congress rejected similar exemptions in 1947 and again in 1974, so the Court amended rather than construed the statute.","disposition":"Affirmed (denial of enforcement). Chief Justice Burger wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Stewart, Powell, Rehnquist and Stevens; Justice Brennan's dissent was joined by Justices White, Marshall and Blackmun.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep440/usrep440490/usrep440490.pdf"},"1978/77-5781":{"facts":"Police received a radio report of a clothing-store robbery and stopped a car they thought was the getaway vehicle. The owner was driving, and petitioners were passengers. After ordering the occupants out, officers searched the car and found rifle shells in the locked glove compartment and a sawed-off rifle under the front passenger seat. Petitioners were arrested and later convicted of armed robbery, with the rifle and shells admitted at trial. Petitioners did not own the car and never claimed to own the rifle or shells.","procedure":"The Illinois trial court denied petitioners' motion to suppress because they lacked standing to challenge the search of the car, and the Illinois Appellate Court affirmed on the ground that a mere passenger without a proprietary or similar interest in a car lacks standing. The Illinois Supreme Court denied leave to appeal, and the U.S. Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May a criminal defendant who is a passenger in a car, and who claims neither ownership of the car nor of the items seized, invoke the Fourth Amendment and the exclusionary rule to challenge a search of the car, either because the search was 'directed' at him or because he was legitimately on the premises?","holding":"No. Fourth Amendment rights are personal and cannot be asserted vicariously, so the Court rejected the 'target' theory and held that the proper inquiry is whether the defendant's own Fourth Amendment rights were infringed, which turns on whether he had a legitimate expectation of privacy in the place searched. Petitioners, who showed no property or possessory interest in the car or the items seized, had not shown a legitimate expectation of privacy in the glove compartment or the area under the seat.","reasoning":"The Court began from the rule that \"Fourth Amendment rights are personal rights\", and declined to extend standing to anyone against whom a search was directed, citing the administrative difficulties of that approach and the social cost of applying the exclusionary rule more widely. It then concluded that the separate label of standing adds nothing, and that the question is better treated as part of substantive Fourth Amendment doctrine: whether the challenged search infringed an interest of the defendant that the Amendment protects. The Court held that the phrase from Jones v. United States, \"legitimately on premises\", is \"too broad a gauge for measurement of Fourth Amendment rights\", because it would let a casual visitor challenge a search of areas in which he had no legitimate expectation of privacy. Relying on Katz, it explained that Jones is best understood as a case in which the defendant had a legitimate expectation of privacy in the apartment he was using, and it stressed that property law concepts are relevant but not controlling. Applying this test, petitioners asserted no property or possessory interest in the car or the seized items, and as mere passengers they did not show a legitimate expectation of privacy in the glove compartment or under the seat. The Court distinguished Jones and Katz on their facts and said it had not held that only a common-law property interest suffices.","separate":"Justice Powell, joined by Chief Justice Burger, concurred. He read the Court's opinion as turning on legitimate expectations of privacy rather than property concepts, stressed that expectations of privacy in an automobile differ from those in a home, and concluded that these passengers, who had no control of the car or keys, had no reasonable expectation that the car would not be searched after they were stopped and made to leave it. Justice White, joined by Justices Brennan, Marshall, and Stevens, dissented. He argued that the Court in effect ties the Fourth Amendment to property law, that Jones and later cases protect a person legitimately present in a private place with the owner's permission, that the Court's rule is not an easily applied bright line, and that it invites police to search vehicles carrying more than one occupant without fear of suppression. He agreed with the Court's rejection of target standing.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Powell, and Blackmun (5-4).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep439/usrep439128/usrep439128.pdf"}});